// The Codex book "heroes": each page as blocks (see lore-codex.py).

import type { CodexPage } from './codex';

export const PAGES: Record<string, CodexPage> = {"heroes/ada": {"title": "Ada, the Artificer", "sub": "The Underforge · [Kilnborn](peoples/kiln) · from [the Deep](realms/deep)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Ada was born in the seventy-fourth summer after the Fall in a workshop above the Underforge's great lift, the daughter of the lift's engineer: the woman who kept the iron cages running up and down the shaft from the hilltops to the forge halls on water and counterweights. Ada was born grey, as every Kilnborn child of her generation was, and grew up in the noise of the lift, with grease to her elbows by five, taking apart anything that would come apart. At eight she rebuilt the workshop's clock so that it ran on the lift's counterweights and told the time by the slip. At ten she built a mechanical mouse that cleaned the cages. At twelve she asked to be apprenticed at the great forge, and her mother, who knew what was good for her, sent her to Brannoc."], ["p", "[Brannoc](heroes/brannoc), the Ashen Smith, taught her everything about metal: how to draw, temper and fold iron in the sun's own fire, how to read a blade's colour as it cools, how to listen to an anvil. He taught her nothing about patience, because he had none to teach. She was his apprentice for eight years, and his best, and the most argumentative person in the forge, which was saying a great deal. In her eighth year, given a hammer to forge as her journeyman piece, she built a hammer that swung itself: a forge hammer with a clockwork heart and a spring of Caldera steel, which would strike, lift and strike again on its own, at the right weight, for an hour. Brannoc looked at it for a long time, and said, \"That's not smithing,\" and Ada said, \"No, it's better,\" and walked out of the forge and set up on her own."], ["p", "She is the Underforge's artificer now, the Master of Engines in the Hall of Marks: a slim inventor in a copper coat, with a backpack of brass gears and goggles pushed up on her leather cap, who builds turrets, drones and hooks faster than she can name them. Most of the Underforge's lifts, pumps and lamps are hers, and she rebuilt her mother's great lift when her mother retired, so that it now runs on steam from the Caldera and carries a hundred people at once. Her workshop hangs over the shaft on iron struts, and she works with the door open so she can hear the lift. In the Ring she fights with what she built that morning: give her ten seconds and something to bolt it to."], ["p", "She thinks the Heart of Noon is the finest engine the Dial has ever had, and she would like to run the Deep on it. Not burn it, as Brannoc wants, and not hang it in the sky, as the Noonsworn want: harness it, with pipes and turbines and gears, so that its heat drives every lift and pump and lamp from the Deep to the Ring. She has drawn the plans. They are very large. Brannoc says it is not smithing. Ada says it is better."], ["p", "She argues engines with [Professor Puff](heroes/professor-puff), the Windward's aeronaut, at every Truce dinner: two inventors at one table, seated across from each other by Madame Cleaver on purpose. She wins every argument about engines, and he wins every one about flying, and they have argued for six years whether a balloon is an engine. She has secretly built a balloon. It works. She has not told him, because then he would win."], ["p", "In the hundredth summer she has been asked by the Hall of Marks to look at the Underforge's drill, the shaft it is boring from the Caldera toward the Hall of Noon, which has slowed in rock that the drill-heads will not bite. She went down it alone with a lamp and a notebook, and came back up two days later with three slivers of glass in her pocket and a face nobody had seen on her before."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Kilnborn of the great lift, born after the Fall: twenty-six, slim and quick, with a bobbed head of red hair, goggles pushed up on a leather cap, a copper coat, and a backpack of brass gears that hum."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Fast, clipped and technical; she thinks aloud, in numbers, and interrupts herself. She is cheerful in a crisis and bored in peace. She names her machines after people she is annoyed with."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Give me ten seconds and something to bolt it to.\"", "**Picked:** \"Stand still. I need to measure you.\"", "**Winning:** \"Works as designed.\"", "**Winning:** \"Bolted down.\"", "**Losing:** \"Prototype. Expected failures.\"", "**Losing:** \"Back to the drawing board. I like the drawing board.\"", "**Meeting Brannoc:** \"Still swinging your own hammer? Charming.\"", "**Meeting Professor Puff:** \"A balloon is a bag, Professor. A bag is not an engine.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["The clockwork heart in her self-swinging hammer runs on a sliver of Caldera glass that still holds a little of the sun's fire. She has three more slivers. She has not told the Hall of Marks where she got them: from the bottom of the Underforge's drill shaft, from very near the Hall of Noon.", "She has built the balloon. It is moored in a cave above the great lift, and she has been up in it six times.", "She is afraid that if the Heart of Noon is harnessed, it will go out. She has run the numbers four times."]], ["h", 2, "What she wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To see the engine inside it. Anything that keeps a sun up, or held one, or drinks one, is a machine, and she would very much like to look at the gears."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Professor Puff](heroes/professor-puff)**, rival: two inventors at one table; she wins every argument about engines, he wins every one about flying.", "**[Brannoc](heroes/brannoc)**, her teacher: he taught her everything about metal, and she built a hammer that swung itself."]]]}, "heroes/anka": {"title": "Anka, the Firebird", "sub": "The Noonsworn · [the Fallborn](peoples/fallborn), an Ember · from [the Drowned Coast](realms/coast)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "When the sun broke at noon on midsummer day a hundred years ago, it threw sparks across the Dial. Most of them went out where they fell: in the Sands, where they turned to glass; in the Wood, where they set small fires the Briarkin put out; in the sea. One of them fell into the surf off the Drowned Coast, a mile south of Sallowmouth, where a girl was dancing on the shore with a fan of gull feathers, as the Coast girls do at midsummer for the turning of the tide. The spark went into the water. It did not go out."], ["p", "The next morning, while the sun rose thin and late from the Tower and the drowned harbour lay under the water, a woman walked out of the surf: barefoot, in ember-red and gold, with a fan of feathers that burned at the tips and did not burn away. She did not know her name. She did not know what she was. She knew how to dance, because the first thing she had seen, from inside the water, was the girl on the shore, and she had taken her shape from her the way an Ember takes its shape from its first sight. The girl had drowned in the flood that afternoon. Anka has carried her face for a hundred years, and has never stopped feeling that she owes it back."], ["p", "The Coast folk called her the sea's fire and were afraid of her. The Noonsworn, when word reached Aubade, called her the sun's promise: proof that what falls can rise. They brought her to the white city and gave her a name, Anka, from an old cliff word for the bird that sings at dawn, and a place among the Friends of the Dawn. She has served the Noonsworn since, gladly. She is not sure she believes everything they do. She believes in rising."], ["p", "Because she does rise. She is fragile, more fragile than she looks: cold hurts her, water held long enough puts her out, and a blade goes into her as into anyone. When she dies, she burns. Her body goes up in a column of fire and leaves embers on the ground, and from the embers, after a time, she rises, whole, about twenty years old in a human's eyes, remembering every life. She always knows where she will come back, because she leaves her embers on the ground for it, and in the Ring she plants them on purpose. She has burned and risen many times since the first morning. Each time it takes a little longer."], ["p", "[Mortis](heroes/mortis), the Ossuary Lord, has buried her three times. He considers it his duty: the dead should stay where they are put, and an Ember that will not stay in the ground offends everything the Long Dead keep. The first time was in year 40, after a duel on the Field of Banners, when he buried her ashes in a lead box under Gloaming's ossuaries; she rose in the box, melted it, and walked out through the Night Temple during the Naming. The second and third times were more careful. She has never stayed. He fights in the Ring for the chance to bury her a fourth time, and she fights him because she will not be kept."], ["p", "The Dune Throne's judge of the dead, the Heartweigher, weighs every royal heart in the Sands against a single feather in her scale, and the feather in her pan, they say, is one of Anka's, picked up from the surf on the morning after the Fall. The Heartweigher would like a fresh one, from the bird herself, and has said so in public. Anka has said that if the Heartweigher wants a feather she can come and take it, and that she will need a very long arm."], ["p", "She climbs to find the rest of the fire she came from: the Heart of Noon, and Ash and Ember in the Cinder Pit, who are, she believes, her elder siblings. She wants to give it back to the sky. She has reached the Wood's crown, and stood in front of Ash and Ember, and they knew her, and she did not fight them. She came down, and burned that night for no reason, and rose late."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "The first and best known of the [Embers](peoples/fallborn), the sparks of the sun that took a shape. She looks twenty: a dancer with warm skin, dark-red hair cut at the jaw, a gold circlet with a three-feather crest and a turquoise stone. An ember-red bodice, a layered skirt of flame-coloured scalloped feathers, a mantle of red and gold feather wings at the arms, an open feather fan in her hand."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Light, quick and teasing, with a laugh just under every sentence; she speaks the Coast's Ring-speech with Aubade's formal words, and talks to fire as if it were a person. When she is about to burn she goes very quiet."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Put me out. Go on. I'll wait.\"", "**Picked:** \"Every fire is the first one.\"", "**Winning:** \"Still burning.\"", "**Winning:** \"Ashes to ashes. Yours, not mine.\"", "**Losing:** \"Mark the spot. I'll be right back.\"", "**Losing:** \"Not out. Just resting.\"", "**Meeting Mortis:** \"A fourth box? You never learn.\"", "**Meeting the Heartweigher:** \"You want a feather? Reach.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["She is slower to rise each time. Twenty heartbeats the first time; nearly a minute now. She thinks that when the old sun's light is gone from the Dial she will not rise at all, and she has not told the Noonsworn.", "She remembers the girl on the shore: her name was Ama, and she was nine. Anka has visited Ama's mother's grave every midsummer.", "Ash and Ember spoke to her at the Wood's crown. They said: \"Not yet.\"", "She is a spark that slipped the Tower's grip when it took the sun's noon. The rest of the fire she came from is not lost: its noon is kept at the Tower's top, over the Noon Seat, and its heart burns under the Ring."]], ["h", 2, "What she wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To find the rest of the fire she came from, and give it back to the sky. And, though she does not say it, to find out whether an Ember can choose to go home."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Mortis](heroes/mortis)**, sworn enemy: he keeps the dead in the ground; she will not stay there. He has buried her three times."]]]}, "heroes/auriel": {"title": "Auriel, the Dawn Paladin", "sub": "The Noonsworn · [Aubade folk](peoples/aubade) · from [Aubade](realms/aubade)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Auriel was born in the bell quarter of Aubade at first light on the morning after the Fall, while the city was still waiting to see whether the sun would rise at all. It rose late and thin, a white coin behind haze, and the bells rang it up for the first time without the choir; and in the house of the White Bell a ringer called Aude gave birth to a daughter and named her for the light. Her father was not there. He was one of the hundred who had sung the Hymn of Noon at the Tower's top the day before, and he was not one of the two who came down."], ["p", "The Noonsworn took her birth for a sign, which they were doing with everything that year. A child born to a choir widow, on the first morning, at the first bell: they wrote it in the book of the dawn, and when Aude died of the grey fever in the third winter, they took the child into the White Barracks to be raised as a paladin. Nobody yet understood what it meant to be Noon-touched. They found out slowly. Auriel's year-mates grew up around her; she did not. At twenty she looked four, at fifty she looked ten. She learned the sword from a paladin who grew old teaching her, and then from his pupil, and then from his pupil's pupil, and she outlived all three of them as a child. She says the Barracks taught her patience. The paladins who served with her say it taught her to be alone in a full room."], ["p", "She was sworn at seventy-five, when she looked fifteen: the youngest-looking paladin the order ever took and, by years, one of the oldest. They put the dawn line on her brow at the first bell and gave her the wings. The wings of the paladins are armour, not feathers: white plates hinged on a frame across the back, made in imitation of the honour guard who once walked the sun's road across the sky, or so the order says. In Auriel they look, everyone agrees, as if they had grown there."], ["p", "Her first years under oath were the Truce's ordinary work: escorting the bell-founders' caravans down the Dawn Stair, guarding the Ember of Aubade in its lantern-shrine, standing at the Ring's gate on midsummer as the Noonsworn's champion-in-waiting. Her first duel in the Ring was in the eightieth summer, against a Dune Throne champion who had called the sun the Father's property. She won it in thirty heartbeats and then helped him up, which the crowd liked and the Noonsworn elders did not. Her shields, the Ring noticed that day, burst outward when they break: a smith's trick of the order, which in her hands became a habit and then a creed. *Every night ends. I am how.*"], ["p", "The night she means is the Court of Dusk's. In the eighty-sixth summer a Noonsworn bell caravan was found on the night road below Gloaming with its ringers alive, pale and asleep, and two of them never woke. The Court said wolves. Auriel rode to the castle-city alone and demanded a champion, under the Truce, at the Ring. The Countess came herself. [Vesper](heroes/vesper) and Auriel have fought eleven times since, five to five with one called by the Arbiter for darkness, and neither has said the other's name kindly once. Auriel believes Vesper is everything the long dusk has made of the Dial: a thing that should have ended and found a way not to. She also knows, and will not say, that Vesper is the only person in the Ring who fights her as an equal and not as a sign."], ["p", "The Noonsworn have given her the duty she wanted most and feared most. When the Sunbearer took up the Ember of Aubade and swore never to set it down except to fight, it was Auriel who put the oath to him, and Auriel who walks in front of him wherever he carries it. If the Noonsworn win the climb, the plan is simple: Auriel opens the way, the Sunbearer carries the Ember up the Tower, and at the Noon Seat it is hung, and the sun rises whole. She has said it so often that it sounds like a prayer."], ["p", "She has rivals among the living. [Sefu](heroes/sefu) of the Sands says the sun is his father's, and Auriel has told him in public that the sun is nobody's father; he answered that she has never met hers either, and she has not forgiven it. And the stone-winged guard, the stone-winged watcher of the Night Temple, waits on high places for her to tire, because her dawn turns him back toward stone; she has never yet tired first."], ["p", "Now, in the hundredth summer, the Tower's gate is open to champions of the Ring, and Auriel has more right to climb than almost anyone. She has gone up as far as the Sea's crown and come down again to wait for the Sunbearer. People ask her what she will do if the sun is hung and the Noon-touched begin to age again. She says she will grow up. She smiles when she says it."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Aubade folk, the people of the morning cliffs, and a sworn paladin of the Noonsworn. She looks twenty and is a hundred. Tall and straight-backed, as Aubade children are taught; warm skin; long hair of dark gold bound with gold thread, one thread for each oath kept, which by now is most of her hair. The dawn line runs gold down her brow. She wears white and gold plate with a halo ring of gold above her helm, a kite shield on her left arm and a sword in her right, and the wings of plates across her back."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Clear and carrying, made for battlefields and bell towers; she speaks in full sentences and in oaths, and rarely raises her voice because she rarely needs to. She laughs seldom, but all the way. To those she trusts she is dry and a little tired. To the Court of Dusk she is very polite."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"First light. Stand behind me.\"", "**Picked:** \"The oath of the dawn holds. Let us see if you do.\"", "**Winning:** \"There. Morning.\"", "**Winning:** \"Get up. The sun does, every day.\"", "**Losing:** \"Night is long. It is not forever.\"", "**Losing:** \"Write it down. I will be back at first bell.\"", "**Meeting Vesper:** \"Countess. You look tired. Shall I let some light in?\"", "**Meeting the Sunbearer:** \"Keep the Ember close. I will keep the rest away.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["The Hourkeepers have a theory, which they have told nobody but the Noonsworn elders and Auriel: if a sun hangs over the Tower again and the hours run true, the Noon-touched may age all at once. Auriel would be a hundred. She has decided to carry the sun up anyway, and has told no one she knows.", "Her father's name was Ansel, a tenor of the choir. [Cantor](heroes/cantor) knew him and has never told her that he was the one who missed the beat. He is not sure it is true. She does not know Cantor knows anything.", "She has met Vesper once outside the Ring, on a night road, without armour, and they talked until morning. Neither has told the Court or the Noonsworn."]], ["h", 2, "What she wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To climb with the Sunbearer to the Noon Seat and hang the sun there, whole, so the hours run true. And, if the Sun Warden still keeps his post at the Tower's top, to ask him one thing: whether the Hymn of Noon was still being sung when the sun fell, and whose voice was the last."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Vesper](heroes/vesper)**, sworn enemy: the dawn and the countess who would keep it from coming. Eleven duels, five to five, and one night on a road that neither will speak of.", "**[Sefu](heroes/sefu)**, rival: who owns the sun. The sky, says the Paladin; my father, says the King. He told her she never met hers."]]]}, "heroes/barry-cloudsley": {"title": "Barry Cloudsley, the Sky Augur", "sub": "The Windward · [Coast folk](peoples/coast) · from [the Drowned Coast](realms/coast)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Barry Cloudsley was born in Saltreach thirty years before the Fall, the son of a sailmaker, and the Cloudsleys have been reading the sky since there was a Saltreach: the name is a weather name, given to a great-grandfather who forecast the great gale of a hundred and twenty years before the Fall a week early and was not believed. Barry was a careful, cheerful, round-faced boy who kept a weather diary from the age of six, and at twenty the Windward's augur on the Weather Spire, an old woman called Nan Gullwhistle, took him on as her junior, because his diary was better than hers."], ["p", "Before the Fall the augur's work was a craft: the sky kept appointments, the seasons came in order, and a good augur could tell a fishing fleet tomorrow's weather from today's clouds and be right nine times in ten. Barry was right nine and a half. He was thirty and calling the noon forecast from the Weather Spire's balcony on the day of the Fall, and he remembers that he had just said \"fair, with a light westerly\", when the sun stopped."], ["p", "Since then the augur's work has been the hardest job on the Dial. The sky no longer keeps its own appointments. Storms come when they like, the winds keep no seasons, and a forecast holds, Barry has measured, for about eight seconds. He was the Spires' junior augur when the great storm of the twenty-third summer came, and he forecast it a day early, and the Council of Captains did not believe him, and ships went onto the Spires that night, which the Coast remembers as the Night of Lanterns. Nan Gullwhistle died the next year and left him the Weather Spire. He has never been disbelieved since. He has, quite often, been wrong."], ["p", "He stands on the highest Spire in a long storm-slate coat with a cloud mantle on his shoulders, a high gold-rimmed collar, windswept hair, and a weathervane staff taller than he is with a glowing orb at the top, and he calls the forecast every eight seconds, which is as long as any forecast now holds. He is right about the weather more often than anyone alive and still mostly wrong, which he blames, loudly, on [Kaia](heroes/kaia). In the Ring his weather hits both heroes, and he is the only one who knows what is coming. That is his whole art: not to change the sky but to know it a moment sooner."], ["p", "The forecast is called from the Weather Spire by bell and by flag, so that every ship in sight of the Spires can read it, and copied by kite to the harbour towns, where boys chalk it on boards at the quays; by the time the chalk is dry it is wrong, and everyone knows it, and everyone reads it anyway. Barry keeps every forecast he has ever called, one hundred and forty million of them by his count, in ledgers in the Spire's lower rooms, and compares them each night with what the sky actually did. The comparison is the most complete record of the masterless weather on the Dial, and the Hourkeepers send a Reader every year to copy it."], ["p", "He and Kaia have argued in public for twelve years, since the Kite Day she called a thunderstorm on a day he had forecast clear. He says she breaks the sky; she says she improves his forecasts. The Coast follows it like a play. He considers her the most gifted weather-worker in a century and has never once said so."], ["p", "His friends are men who watch the sky. [Bo](heroes/bo), the hill shepherd of the Moot, trusts his forecasts, which makes one of them: they have written to each other every month for thirty years, Barry's forecasts for Bo's fleece-readings, and Bo says Barry is right more often than the sheep. And [Professor Puff](heroes/professor-puff), the aeronaut, his oldest flying companion: one reads the sky, the other maps it, and they have gone up together in the Professor's basket more times than either can count, Barry calling the wind and the Professor steering into it."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Coast folk of Saltreach, Noon-touched: thirty at the Fall, he looks fifty, with a calm round face under windswept brown hair. A storm-slate coat with a cloud mantle and a high gold-rimmed collar, the weathervane staff with its glowing orb in the left hand."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Warm, measured and cheerful, a professional forecaster's voice that has delivered bad news to sailors for seventy years. He speaks in forecasts (\"rain later, followed by you falling over\") and laughs at his own jokes, which are all about weather."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"And now, the weather. You won't like it.\"", "**Picked:** \"Outlook: unsettled. For you.\"", "**Winning:** \"As forecast.\"", "**Winning:** \"Clearing up nicely.\"", "**Losing:** \"Unexpected. Nobody could have predicted it. Except me.\"", "**Losing:** \"A brief shower. Of me.\"", "**Meeting Kaia:** \"Don't touch the sky, Kaia. I've just forecast it.\"", "**Meeting Bo:** \"Rain by noon, Bo. Tell the sheep.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["He believes the storms that come off the Sands are called by someone in Tamerit. He is half right: some of them were Kaia's, sold to a Dune house. He has begun to suspect, and would rather not know.", "Since the Climb opened, his forecasts have been right more often. He thinks the Tower is taking the sky's weather into itself, floor by floor, and leaving the Dial's sky emptier.", "He has never been up in a storm. Every flight with the Professor, he has forecast clear skies, and made sure."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To stand on top of it and read the sky from above the clouds, the one place no augur has ever stood; and to find out where the weather went."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Kaia](heroes/kaia)**, rival: he forecasts the weather and she changes it to prove him wrong; twelve years of public argument.", "**[Bo](heroes/bo)**, friend: shepherds watch the sky, and Bo is the one man on the Dial who trusts Barry's forecasts.", "**[Professor Puff](heroes/professor-puff)**, friend: his oldest flying companion: one reads the sky, the other maps it."]]]}, "heroes/big-yoko": {"title": "Big Yoko, the Mountain", "sub": "The Truce of the Ring · [Aubade folk](peoples/aubade) · from [Aubade](realms/aubade)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Yoko was born on the shrine cliffs below Aubade, eighteen years before the Fall, to a family of shrine wrestlers: the men and women who train in the straw-rope rings cut into the cliff terraces at dawn and wrestle at the festivals for the honour of the bell towers. He was a giant from the cradle. At ten he was taller than his father; at fourteen he could lift the shrine's bronze bell off its hook. He was also, everyone agreed, the gentlest child on the cliffs, and he hated to win."], ["p", "The shrine wrestlers have a teaching: the ring is small, the world outside it is large, and a wrestler chooses which to live in. A bout is won by pushing the other out of the ring, not by hurting them, and the loser is not beaten but returned to the world. Yoko took it more seriously than anyone. He wrestled to show that a giant can be pushed out by a quicker hand, and he kept hoping someone would do it."], ["p", "At eighteen he went to the midsummer market at the Tower's foot to wrestle in its straw-rope circle, which every Aubade wrestler did once. He was warming up in it when the sun stopped, cracked and fell. The ground jumped; he was thrown off his feet. When he got up the market was gone, the dust was falling, and a mason's boy with a broom was standing at the circle's edge, not crying. Yoko stood in the circle, because it was the one thing that was still where it had been, and he did not leave it."], ["p", "Through the two years of the War of the Banners he stood in it. Knights came to the circle to settle quarrels one against one instead of with their armies; Yoko was the circle's boundary, and nobody fought outside the rope while he stood there. He threw salt before every bout to keep the circle clean, as the shrine wrestlers do. He ate what [Madame Cleaver](heroes/madame-cleaver) brought him from the camps across the Field. When the realms came to swear the Truce, they swore it round his circle, and the Ring was built on it."], ["p", "He is the Truce's first champion and its living boundary: the ring stamped on the floor of every duel is drawn after the one he defends. He came to the Ring to lose, and nobody has managed it since the Ring was opened, which is the great sorrow of his life. Every year at the Ring's opening he pushes the same great stone against [Bram](heroes/bram) of the Underforge, and neither has moved it farther than the other. He considers Bram his closest rival and the only man on the Dial he cannot read."], ["p", "He throws salt before every bout, still, and the salt ends up all over the Ring's floor, which [Gus](heroes/gus), the keeper of the sea temple, has asked him to stop many times. Gus sweeps it up. Yoko throws it again. Each thinks the other is very patient, and they are friends."], ["p", "He lives, as far as anyone can tell, in the Ring. He has a room under the east stand with a bed that Bram's masons built for him out of one slab of limestone, and he trains at dawn in the empty circle while the Saltwarden salts its edge, the two of them working in silence. Children of the Ringside come to watch, and he lets them try to push him out, one at a time and then all at once. They never manage it. He always thanks them. In the hundredth summer the Climb has drawn champions from every realm past his door, and many of them stop to salute him on the way to the gate, as if he were part of the Tower. He salutes them back, and asks them, every one, to come down again."], ["p", "He fights because the Ring is a promise, and a promise needs someone to stand in it."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Aubade folk of the shrine cliffs: by far the widest hero in the Ring, huge in the shoulder and the belly, with warm skin, a calm round face and a topknot tied in gold. A straw-rope belt with paper charms, a crimson stole across the chest, crimson lacquered plates on the shoulders, bare feet."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Deep, slow and kind, with long silences. He speaks in proverbs from the shrine cliffs and means every one. He thanks his opponents before and after every bout."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"The ring is small. The world outside it is large. Choose.\"", "**Picked:** \"Salt first. Then we begin.\"", "**Winning:** \"Back to the world with you. Gently.\"", "**Winning:** \"Out. Thank you for the bout.\"", "**Losing:** \"Ah. At last. Thank you.\"", "**Losing:** \"The ring is still here. So am I.\"", "**Meeting Bram:** \"The stone again? Of course the stone again.\"", "**Meeting Gus:** \"I'll sweep it myself this time. I promise.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["He did lose, once: in year 3, in an exhibition bout, to a girl of the Coast who stepped round him while he was saluting her. She stepped outside the rope to do it, and the Arbiter let it stand. Yoko has been grateful ever since, and never told anyone it counted.", "He believes the circle he stands in is the exact place where the Tower's shadow stood at the moment of the Fall, and that if he ever leaves it, the shadow will move.", "He cannot read. Gus reads him his letters."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "Nothing for himself. He would like whoever climbs it to come back down and step out of the ring when it is time."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Madame Cleaver](heroes/madame-cleaver)**, friend: she has fed the Mountain since he was a boy; nobody else owns a pot big enough.", "**[Bram](heroes/bram)**, rival: every year at the Ring's opening they push the same stone, and neither has moved it farther than the other.", "**[Gus](heroes/gus)**, friend: Yoko throws the salt and Gus sweeps it up, and both think the other is very patient."]]]}, "heroes/bo": {"title": "Bo, the Ramherd", "sub": "The Greenwood Moot · [Wood folk](peoples/wood), ram-horned · from [the Walking Wood](realms/wood)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Bo was born in a shepherds' summer town in the high pastures above the Wood, forty years before the Fall, and has herded sheep for as long as he can remember. The high pastures are where the forest gives way to the hills, open grass and limestone and wind, with the Wood a dark line below and the Tower a grey needle on the eastern horizon; the hill families drive their flocks up in spring and down in autumn, and keep to themselves, and are reckoned slow by the valley folk because they think before they speak. Bo thinks longer than most."], ["p", "He was forty and his beard was just going silver on the day of the Fall. He was on the high ridge with three hundred ewes when the sun stopped over the Tower, and he watched it fall from the best seat on the Dial. He says he does not remember being afraid. He remembers the sheep all turning, at the same moment, to face the Tower, and standing quite still, and then all turning back and going on grazing. He decided then that the sheep knew something, and he has watched them more carefully ever since."], ["p", "The Wood began to walk below him, and the hill families found that the forest's edge came up the slopes in places, and went down in others, so that a pasture might be a wood next summer and a wood a pasture. The flocks followed the grass. Bo followed the flocks, and learned to read where the Wood would walk next by where the sheep would not go. When the Moot gathered after the War of the Banners to decide what the Wood folk would do about the walking, the hill families sent Bo to speak for them, because nobody else would sit still that long. He swore the Moot's oath the same year. In the hills the oath grows ram horns, and his came in a great curl from each temple within the year; he wears a bronze headband between them that his wife made him, and has worn it since she died."], ["p", "He is a herdsman-druid of the old hill kind: he knows the weather by the fleece, the season by the lambs and the Wood's mood by the sheep, and the hill workings, which are slow and patient and mostly to do with getting large animals to go where you want. He carries a crook hung with a bell that his sheep will follow anywhere. Really they follow whoever is nearest, which he will tell you is a lesson about leaders. He sits on the Moot because somebody has to speak for the hills, and he speaks slowly, and the Moot has learned that when Bo starts a sentence it should wait for the end of it."], ["p", "His friends are the Wood's other quiet men. [Hank](heroes/hank), the woodcutter, with whom he has shared a fire, a flock (the year Hankshold's sheep were caught in a walking grove and Bo brought them out) and a great many silences. And [Barry Cloudsley](heroes/barry-cloudsley), the Windward's augur on the Spires, whose forecasts Bo trusts, which makes one of them: shepherds watch the sky, and Bo has compared Barry's forecasts to his fleece for thirty years and says Barry is right more often than the sheep, which is high praise. They write to each other every month. Barry sends forecasts; Bo sends wool."], ["p", "He fights in the Ring when a quarrel reaches his pastures, and sends the flock in first. The flock is real, mostly, and the rams are very real, and very large, and the Briarkin worked something into the bell long ago that nobody fully understands."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Wood folk of the high pastures, sworn to the Moot and ram-horned, and Noon-touched: forty at the Fall, he looks sixty. A tidy silver beard under a calm weathered face with level grey brows and steady eyes, grey hair under a bronze headband, a great ram horn curling from each temple, a deep umber wool cloak with a grey fleece mantle, and the crook in the left hand with its glowing warm bell."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Slow, warm and deep, with the hill accent and long pauses in the middle of sentences. He addresses everyone as if they were a nervous sheep, kindly. He has never been heard to hurry."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Easy now. They're only sheep. Mostly.\"", "**Picked:** \"Up the hill we go. Slowly.\"", "**Winning:** \"Into the fold with you.\"", "**Winning:** \"Steady. Steady. There.\"", "**Losing:** \"Scattered. They'll come back. They always do.\"", "**Losing:** \"The hill's still there. So's the flock.\"", "**Meeting Hank:** \"Bring the cider. I've brought the silence.\"", "**Meeting Barry Cloudsley:** \"What's the sky say, Barry? The fleece says rain.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["The sheep turned to face the Tower on the day of the Fall, and they do it again, all of them, every year at noon on midsummer, and stand still for the space of nine heartbeats. Bo has counted. The Hourkeepers would want to know, and he has not told them.", "His wife was a Briarkin of the Acorn court who chose to live in the hills and grow old. She died in year 61. Pim and Pom are, he suspects, her kin.", "The bell on his crook was hers."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To know what the sheep know. He thinks they are listening for something at noon, and that the answer is at the top."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Hank](heroes/hank)**, friend: they have shared a fire, a flock and a great many silences.", "**[Barry Cloudsley](heroes/barry-cloudsley)**, friend: shepherds watch the sky, and Bo is the one man on the Dial who trusts Barry's forecasts."]]]}, "heroes/bram": {"title": "Bram, the Stonewright", "sub": "The Underforge · [Kilnborn](peoples/kiln) · from [the Deep](realms/deep)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Bram was born in the masons' halls of the Deep eighteen years before the Fall, the son of a mason, the grandson of a mason, and so back further than the family can count: his family's maker's mark, a square with a dot in its corner, is cut on pillars in the Deep's oldest halls, the ones the mole-folk say they dug first. He was a broad, calm, slow-spoken boy who liked stone because it waited. He learned to cut at six, to dress at ten, to set at fourteen, and to build at sixteen, and he has built the halls of the Underforge stone by stone ever since."], ["p", "He has one friend, and the friend is a pillar. Gerald is a limestone pillar about as tall as Bram, with a carved face, a grumpy one, on one side. Bram found him at eighteen, on the day of the Fall, in the oldest gallery of the masons' halls, where the shaking of the rock had brought down a wall and exposed a pillar that was not on any plan, older than the masons' oldest marks, older than the Deep, Bram says, with a face cut into it that nobody had cut. Bram put his hand on it to steady himself as the ground moved, and the face looked at him, and said, quite clearly, \"Mind your feet.\" Nobody else has ever heard Gerald speak. Bram has never doubted him."], ["p", "Gerald goes everywhere with him. Bram carries him, or rolls him, or, in the Ring, sets him down, and Gerald stands there being stone in the most useful place possible: a wall, a pillar to hide behind, a stone that topples. Bram is broad and calm, with carved limestone pauldrons, a leather apron, a rune-cut maul and a glowing amber rune on his chest, his mark, cut on his apron's bib in a gold ring. \"Gerald says hello,\" he says before a bout, and, after, \"Gerald says goodbye,\" and he is not joking."], ["p", "He carved the ledge on the Night Temple's eaves in Gloaming in the fiftieth summer, for a statue the Court commissioned and never made, and twenty years later the Court set a guard turned to stone on that ledge, and at dusk the guard woke: the stone-winged guard. Bram went to Gloaming to see what had happened to his ledge, and found the stone had grown into the guard and the guard into the stone, and that the stone-winged guard had wings Bram had carved in the niche. They are, oddly, friends. Bram visits once a year, and they talk on the eaves until morning about the grain of rock."], ["p", "Every year at the Ring's opening he pushes the same great stone against [Big Yoko](heroes/big-yoko), the Mountain: a block of the Ring's own foundation that the founders set in the sand in year 3 as a test of strength. Neither has ever moved it farther than the other. Bram considers Yoko the only man on the Dial who understands that a stone is not an enemy."], ["p", "Two of his fellow craftsmen offend him. The Golemwright, the golem-maker, writes words on the brows of clay and iron bodies and makes them obey: Bram thinks a friend should be made of stone and should choose you, and that a servant made of clay who obeys is a sad thing, and the Golemwright thinks that is very sentimental. And the Hallbreaker, the sapper, who knocks down what Bram builds, with powder; he builds what she knocks down, and she knocks down what he builds, and they have rebuilt and re-demolished the same gallery of the old Deep four times out of principle."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Kilnborn of the masons' halls, and Noon-touched: eighteen at the Fall, he looks thirty-eight. Broad and calm, with a square-jawed face under iron-grey hair, carved limestone pauldrons, a leather apron with the amber rune of his mark glowing on its bib, a rune-cut stone maul; and Gerald."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Slow, deep and even, with long pauses; he speaks for Gerald as well as himself, and Gerald is ruder. He never raises his voice. He talks to stone as if it were listening, because he thinks it is."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Gerald says hello. Gerald says goodbye.\"", "**Picked:** \"Stand behind Gerald. He doesn't mind.\"", "**Winning:** \"Set in stone.\"", "**Winning:** \"Gerald says you were very brave.\"", "**Losing:** \"Cracked. Stone cracks. It doesn't break.\"", "**Losing:** \"Gerald says get up. I'm getting up.\"", "**Meeting Big Yoko:** \"The stone again, Mountain. Same as every year.\"", "**Meeting the Hallbreaker:** \"Don't you dare. I just finished that.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["Gerald has told him what the pillar was before the Deep was dug: a marker, one of nine, set in a ring round the Tower's foot, before anyone remembers. Bram has looked for the other eight. He has found three, one of them under the Ring.", "He can hear stone, a little, as the stone-winged guard can. The Tower's stone is silent. It frightens him.", "He let the Hallbreaker win the gallery the fourth time."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To touch its stone and find out who cut it. Every stone has a mason; even Gerald. Bram has never seen a seam in the Tower, and he would like to know how it was done."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Big Yoko](heroes/big-yoko)**, rival: every year at the Ring's opening they push the same stone, and neither has moved it farther than the other."]]]}, "heroes/brannoc": {"title": "Brannoc, the Ashen Smith", "sub": "The Underforge · [Kilnborn](peoples/kiln) · from [the Deep](realms/deep)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Brannoc was born in the southern hills thirty years before the Fall, the son of a smith in a hill town over the Deep, when the hill people were still called hillmen and nobody's skin was grey. He was a master smith by twenty-five, a huge, ash-bearded, short-tempered man who could draw a sword blade in a morning and argue about it all afternoon. The quarry above his town was a cold crater then, where the hill people cut stone."], ["p", "On the day of the Fall the crater filled with fire from below. Brannoc was at his anvil and felt the ground warm under his feet, and went out and stood at the crater's edge and watched the fallen sun's heat come up through the rock: a lake of slow fire where the quarry had been, cooling at the edges into black glass. Everyone else ran. Brannoc fetched his hammer. Within a month he had built a forge on the crater's rim and was working iron in the sun's own fire, and iron worked in that fire, he found, came out like nothing anyone had ever made: harder, brighter, and warm for days. The hill people followed him down to the heat, and built the great forge round the **Ember Caldera**, and called themselves the Underforge."], ["p", "The fire changed them, as it changed him. Over a generation the hill people's skin went grey as ash and their eyes pale as a coal's edge; they began to be called the Kilnborn. Brannoc went further. In the third year after the Fall, when the great forge's first furnace would not draw, he opened his own chest to the Caldera's fire, by a working nobody has repeated, and took a coal of it in. A forge glows in his chest now. It is why he is called the Ashen Smith. He has no cooldowns, only heat: every working he does heats him, and at white heat he burns, and must quench. Every Kilnborn smith since has been grey; Brannoc is grey and cracked, with lines of ember light across his chest and arms where the forge shows through."], ["p", "He is the Master of the great forge, sunk in the Ember Caldera, where the fire of the fallen sun still rises through the rock. The Ring's iron is his, and the Gaol's chains, and half the blades on the Dial. He taught [Ada](heroes/ada) everything about metal and nothing about patience, and she built a hammer that swung itself, and he has never forgiven her for it or been prouder of anything."], ["p", "He has loved two women. In the war, [Madame Cleaver](heroes/madame-cleaver), the cook who fed both armies from one fire: in year 30 her cleaver came to his forge for a new edge, and went back to her with a promise neither of them kept, to open an inn together at the Waymeet when the war was really over. And in year 40, [Isolde](heroes/isolde), the Winter Warden of Frostfall. He followed her north, fire to her frost, and set up his forge on the shore of Frostfall Lake, and the ice cracked, and the library under it began to drip. She froze his forge solid to send him home. It took him a year to light it again. She says it was the kindest thing she knew how to do. He has not followed anyone since, and neither of them has married."], ["p", "He wants the Ember of Aubade for his fire. The Noonsworn's last piece of the old sun, carried on the Sunbearer's back, would burn in the great forge, he believes, until the Caldera's fire was a sun underground, lighting the Deep and warming the Dial. He fights the Sunbearer for it whenever the Ring allows. The Sunbearer respects him, which he finds very annoying."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Kilnborn, of the first generation born grey, and Noon-touched: thirty at the Fall, he looks fifty. Huge, with ash-grey skin that goes darker toward the hands, and pale amber eyes behind the forge visor. The fire in his chest is the Caldera's, taken in on purpose, and its light shows through his skin: ember seams run up from the furnace grate in his breastplate, over the open breast to his throat, and along both arms down to the bracers. An ash beard, iron pauldrons and breastplate, a scorched leather apron, the forge pack with its two chimneys on his back, and the hammer in his right hand. The Ashen Smith's title is literal."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Loud, rough and impatient, a voice made for shouting over furnaces. He speaks in short commands and long complaints. When he is pleased he says nothing at all, and hits something."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Hotter. Again. Hotter.\"", "**Picked:** \"Stand back. I'm working.\"", "**Winning:** \"Forged.\"", "**Winning:** \"Quenched. Next.\"", "**Losing:** \"Cracked in the cooling. My fault.\"", "**Losing:** \"Back in the fire with me.\"", "**Meeting the Sunbearer:** \"Give me the Ember, boy. It wants a proper forge.\"", "**Meeting Isolde:** \"Still cold, Warden? I'm still hot.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["The coal in his chest is going out. The Caldera burns a little lower every year, and so does he. He has measured both. He wants the Ember because he thinks it is the only thing that can relight him.", "He still has the deed to the Waymeet inn, signed by Cleaver. She does not know he kept his copy.", "The Underforge's drill toward the Hall of Noon was his idea."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "The Heart of Noon, below, where it belongs, and its fire brought up through the rock; and if he cannot have that, the Ember, in his forge."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Isolde](heroes/isolde)**, old flame: fire followed frost north once. She froze his forge solid to send him home, and neither has married.", "**[Madame Cleaver](heroes/madame-cleaver)**, old flame: her cleaver came from his forge, and so did a promise neither of them kept.", "**[Ada](heroes/ada)**, her teacher: he taught her everything about metal, and she built a hammer that swung itself."]]]}, "heroes/cantor": {"title": "Cantor, the Last Verse", "sub": "The Noonsworn · [Aubade folk](peoples/aubade) · from [Aubade](realms/aubade)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "The sun was held up, the old songs of Aubade say, by the Hymn of Noon, sung every midday from the Tower's top by a choir of a hundred. Cantor was born thirty years before the Fall in the bell quarter of Aubade, into a family that had sent a singer to the choir in every generation for three hundred years, and he never wanted to be anything else. He had a light, true baritone and an infallible sense of time: as a boy he could clap with the bells of all hundred towers at once and never miss. The choir took him at sixteen."], ["p", "For fourteen years he climbed the Tower every morning with the ninety-nine and sang the Hymn at noon in the Sun Court, under the great dial, with the Sun Warden standing at his post and the sun overhead like a bell about to ring. The Hymn was a beat before it was a song: the choir's master kept time with a staff on the floor, and a note off the beat went sour. Cantor never sang one. He sat at the master's left hand. Beside him sat a young soprano called Mum, from a family of ringers, who sang so purely that the Sun Warden was said to turn his head when she came in, and whom Cantor loved for nine years without telling her."], ["p", "On the day of the Fall the choir sang the Hymn at noon, and the sun stopped overhead and cracked and fell anyway. Cantor remembers the moment exactly, because he has had a hundred years to remember it: the master's staff came down on the beat, the choir came in, and somewhere in the second line, somewhere to his left, someone was a hair behind. Then the light went white and then went out. When he could see again the Sun Court was empty except for the Warden at his post and Mum on her knees beside him, and the ninety-eight were gone. Not dead; gone. The two of them came down the Tower's stair together in the dark. She did not speak on the way down. She has not made a sound since."], ["p", "He is the last who knows every verse of the Hymn but one: the last verse, which only the master knew, and which was sung only at midsummer. He teaches it in the Choir Loft of Aubade, where the hundred chairs stand with lilies on ninety-eight of them, to children who will never sing it at the Tower's top. He sings on the beat, because the Hymn was a beat before it was a song, and he fights on it: in the Ring his workings come one to a beat, and off the beat they go sour. He has walked the Field of Banners singing the dead armies their marching songs, and the ashen soldiers of the Climb's War, he has heard, keep step when he sings."], ["p", "He has never stopped singing to Mum. She came down from the Tower in a porcelain mask, went to Gloaming and the Court of Dusk, and became its harlequin. He goes to Gloaming every midwinter for the Masque of Houses and sings under her window. She has never answered, and she has never left the room. The bell-founder the Bellwright is his closest friend: her bells keep his beat, and his Hymn tells her where to hang them. She is the only person he has told about the hair behind the beat."], ["p", "He fights in the Ring for the right to climb, to sing the Hymn once more where it was meant to be sung. He has been as far as the Sea's crown and stood in front of the Sun Warden, who looked at him for a long time and then said, in the voice Cantor had heard every noon for fourteen years, \"You are late.\""], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Aubade folk of the bell quarter, Noon-touched: thirty at the Fall, he looks fifty, lean and upright, with a pale face and dark hair under a plumed hat. A rose and plum coat with a lyre strap, a parchment scarf printed with notes, a golden lyre in the left hand."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "A trained baritone, warm and exact; he speaks in measures, and his sentences land on the beat. He hums under his breath when he is thinking, and counts when he is angry."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"On the beat. Always on the beat.\"", "**Picked:** \"One, and two, and...\"", "**Winning:** \"And that's the end of the verse.\"", "**Winning:** \"Perfect time. As it should be.\"", "**Losing:** \"Off the beat. Sour.\"", "**Losing:** \"Rest. Then the next bar.\"", "**Meeting Mum:** \"Still listening? I'm still singing.\"", "**Meeting the Bellwright:** \"Ring me in, the Bellwright.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["The voice a hair behind the beat, somewhere to his left, he has always believed was Ansel, the tenor who sat at Mum's other side: Auriel's father. He has never told Auriel. He is not certain. He thinks about it every noon.", "One of the ninety-eight is in the Climb's Deep, as a ruler: the Blind Cantor, a bass who sat across from him. Cantor does not know.", "He knows Mum knows the last verse. The master taught it to her the week before the Fall. He has never asked her for it."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To stand in the Sun Court and sing the Hymn of Noon once more, every verse, on the beat; and to learn the last verse, from someone, before he does."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Mum](heroes/mum)**, old flame: they sang the Hymn of Noon side by side on the day it failed. She has made no sound since; he has not stopped singing to her."]]]}, "heroes/fenna": {"title": "Fenna, the Grove Warden", "sub": "The Greenwood Moot · [Wood folk](peoples/wood) · from [the Walking Wood](realms/wood)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Fenna was born in the village of Third Glade, deep in the Still Wood, fifteen years before the Fall, the daughter of a forester and a beekeeper. She was a quiet, stubborn girl who talked to trees, as Wood children do, and unlike most Wood children she listened for an answer. At twelve she planted an acorn in her mother's garden and sat by it every evening for a year; the village thought she was simple. At thirteen the oak was taller than she was, which no oak should be in a year, and the village stopped thinking anything aloud."], ["p", "She was fifteen and in the garden on the day of the Fall. She felt the Wood move before she saw it: a shudder through the ground, as of a great animal getting to its feet. Then every tree in Third Glade leaned east, toward the Tower, and pulled. The roots came up out of the soil with a sound like a sail tearing, and the forest began to walk. Third Glade's houses were trampled in an afternoon. Fenna stood in her mother's garden with her hand on her own oak, which had not moved, and told the trees round it to stop, and they did, for a moment, because she asked."], ["p", "That moment made her life. The Wood walked on, and has walked for a hundred years, east toward the Tower, at night mostly, as if it had felt the sun go and set off to find it; and Fenna has spent those hundred years keeping it from trampling the Dial. She swore the Moot's oath at sixteen, the youngest to swear it, and within the year she grew horns: not the antlers of bone that the Moot's hunters grow, but branches, living wood, that put out leaves in spring and blossom in early summer and drop their leaves in the autumn like any tree. The Moot made her its **Grove Warden**, and she has been it ever since."], ["p", "She walks ahead of the Wood. Where a grove is about to walk over a village, she asks it to go round. Where a stand of pines has walked into a river and is drowning, she leads it out. Where the Wood's walking has torn up a field, she plants flowers, because a flower grows faster than a tree and holds the soil until the trees come back. She draws strength from standing among the things she has planted, and in the Ring she plants flowers wherever she fights. She holds the gate of the Climb's Wood, where the forest is still in its first year of walking, and roots rise as cover where she stands."], ["p", "She argues with [Hank](heroes/hank), the Moot's woodcutter, about every tree he fells. He says a tree that walks into a village must be cut; she says it must be asked to leave. He says he asks, with an axe. She calls him a butcher and he calls her a gardener; both are right, and they have shared a fire every winter for twenty years, at a clearing on the Wood's edge where his hut and her garden happen to meet. She has never once won the argument, or lost it."], ["p", "And she has an enemy. In the seventy-first summer, on the Brass Road where it skirts the Wood's south-eastern edge, [Vitriol](heroes/vitriol) of the Dune Throne tested a new fume on a garden by the road: Fenna's, a mile of wildflowers she had planted to hold the soil where the Wood had walked. Every plant died in an hour. He called it a successful test, and wrote to the Moot to thank it for the use of the garden. She has not forgotten. They have fought in the Ring six times, and she has planted a flower on every spot where he fell."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Wood folk, sworn to the Moot at sixteen, and Noon-touched: fifteen at the Fall, she looks thirty-five. A cloak of layered leaves, a pale bark collar, ruddy skin and auburn hair, and branches like antlers growing from her head, living wood, in leaf; a living staff that blooms in her left hand."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Soft, unhurried and certain, the voice of someone used to waiting for things to grow. She speaks to people and plants in the same tone, and expects the same obedience from both. She is never sarcastic, which makes her rare rebukes land hard."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Stand still. Let something grow.\"", "**Picked:** \"Roots first. Then we talk.\"", "**Winning:** \"There. Now something can grow here.\"", "**Winning:** \"Rest. The flowers will keep you company.\"", "**Losing:** \"Cut back. Not cut down.\"", "**Losing:** \"Spring comes. Always.\"", "**Meeting Hank:** \"Put the axe down. Just this once.\"", "**Meeting Vitriol:** \"You owe me a garden, alchemist.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["Her horns are getting longer each year, and the roots of them, she can feel, go deeper. She thinks she is slowly becoming a tree, and that this is what the Moot's oath means. She does not mind as much as she should.", "The oak she planted at twelve walked away with the Wood on the day of the Fall. It is the Elder Root in the Climb's Wood. She has stood in front of it and known it, and it knew her.", "She keeps the root Hank cut from the Tower's wall in a pot in her garden. It has grown a leaf."]], ["h", 2, "What she wants from the Tower"], ["p", "The Tower's roots cut, and the Wood allowed to stop walking. She believes the Wood walks because the Tower is pulling it. And she wants her oak back."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Hank](heroes/hank)**, rival: she grows them, he fells them. They argue every day and share a fire every winter.", "**[Vitriol](heroes/vitriol)**, sworn enemy: his fumes killed a whole garden of hers, and he called it a successful test."]]]}, "heroes/grandma-ironknit": {"title": "Grandma Ironknit, the Weaver", "sub": "The Hourkeepers · [Briarkin](peoples/briar), the Spindle court · from [the Walking Wood](realms/wood)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "The Hourkeepers say every life on the Dial is a thread, and Grandma Ironknit holds the spindle. She is a tiny old fate-spinner in a dark hooded shawl knitted in bands of rose thread, with two needles as long as swords and a grey cat called Mr. Biscuit, and she knits lives into the cloth of the Dial in a stone cottage on the Frostfall road, at the Walking Wood's north-eastern edge. The Hourkeepers have always known what she is. The Wood folk pretend not to. The rest of the Dial thinks she is a very old woman, which she is, in a way: she is a Briarkin of the Spindle court, and she has been old for three hundred years."], ["p", "The Spindle court is the smallest of the Briarkin's courts and the strangest. Its fae sit, wherever they please, with spindles, and spin; and what they spin, the Briarkin say, is the thread of what happens. Not fate, exactly: a Spindle fae cannot make anything happen. But they can see the thread, and follow it, and, if they are very careful, knot it, so that a life that was about to fray holds a little longer. They have silver eyes of one colour, long clever fingers, and great age worn lightly, and they are rarely seen outside the Underbough, because mortals who learn what they do tend to ask for things."], ["p", "A little over two hundred years before the Fall, a Spindle fae chose to live among mortals. Nobody in the Underbough knows why, and she has never said; the Wood folk's story is that she fell in love with a woodcutter, and the Hourkeepers' story is that she wanted to see a thread from close up. She built a stone cottage on the Frostfall road, put a spinning wheel in the window, took the use-name Ironknit (the Spindle court's word for a spinner who has chosen to live among mortals), and began to knit. She has been there since. She was knitting when the sun fell, and she says she felt every thread on the Dial jerk at once, as if someone had pulled the whole cloth, and that she dropped three stitches and has never found them."], ["p", "Within a few years the Hourkeepers came to her door and asked her to keep the threads, as they keep the hours, and she agreed, and became one of them: the Weaver of the Hourkeepers, who keeps the record of lives as the library keeps the record of years. She knits it. Somewhere in her cottage, in a cloth that never ends, is a row for everyone alive on the Dial."], ["p", "In the first winter after the Fall the Underbough left two Briarkin children of the Acorn court on her doorstep, born two for one, one life between them: [Pim and Pom](heroes/pim-and-pom). The courts were frightened. They left them with the one Briarkin they knew who had chosen to live among mortals, and hoped. Ironknit raised them anyway, and named them, and has kept them for ninety-nine years, and loves them more than any thread. She also taught the Sky-Stitcher to sew, a Northern girl who came to her door at eight to learn needlework, and to sew places once cloth was too easy. And fifty years ago [Mr. Ledger](heroes/mr-ledger), the Tithe-Keeper, who has courted nobody in a hundred and sixty years, came to her door and asked to write her into his book: the only time he has ever offered to record a person rather than a debt. She knitted him a scarf instead. He wears it under the collar every day. She knows exactly how much it means, because she knitted it."], ["p", "In the Ring she ties her lines across the floor, rose threads that bind whoever crosses them, and sends the cat."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "A Briarkin of the Spindle court, three hundred years among mortals. Tiny, old and kind, and plainly fae: long pointed ears come out through the rolled rose edge of her hood, her eyes are of one colour, silver from lid to lid, her skin has the faint dapple of light through leaves, and her fingers are a little too long, which is useful for knitting. A dark wine shawl knitted in bands of rose thread with a gold brooch, the hood up round her face and her silver hair, a spindle with three glowing rose spools behind her shoulder, two silver needles as long as swords, and Mr. Biscuit. From the Ring's stands she reads as a small hooded figure with ears, not as another short human."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Warm, slow and comfortable, a grandmother's voice, with something underneath it that is not a grandmother's at all. She calls everyone \"dear\". She counts stitches aloud when she is thinking, in a language nobody else speaks."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Sit down, dear. You're coming unravelled.\"", "**Picked:** \"Knit one. Purl one. Pay attention.\"", "**Winning:** \"Tied off.\"", "**Winning:** \"There. Neat as a sock.\"", "**Losing:** \"Dropped a stitch. Happens.\"", "**Losing:** \"I'll pick it up later. I always do.\"", "**Meeting Mr. Ledger:** \"Still wearing it, dear? Good.\"", "**Meeting Pim and Pom:** \"Which one are you today? Never mind. Come here.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["The three stitches she dropped at the Fall were three lives she could not hold: the choir's master, the Countess of Gloaming's steward's daughter, and a third she will not name. She has looked for them in the cloth for a hundred years. One of them is still alive somewhere, unknitted.", "Pim's and Pom's thread is one thread. If it breaks, it breaks for both. She has knotted it eleven times.", "Mr. Ledger's thread ended sixty years before the Fall. She has been knitting it anyway, out of spare wool, for fifty years."]], ["h", 2, "What she wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To see the Tower's thread. Everything has one. She has looked for the Tower's in the cloth for three hundred years and never found it, and she would like to know whether it is not in the cloth, or whether it is the cloth."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Mr. Ledger](heroes/mr-ledger)**, old flame: fifty years ago he asked to write her into his book; she knitted him a scarf instead, which he still wears.", "**[Pim and Pom](heroes/pim-and-pom)**, their guardian: she found them on her doorstep the winter after the Fall and raised them as her own."]]]}, "heroes/gus": {"title": "Gus, the Temple Keeper", "sub": "The Windward · [Coast folk](peoples/coast) · from [the Drowned Coast](realms/coast)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Gus was born in Saltreach in the thirty-fifth summer after the Fall, the son of a rope-walker and a net-mender, and christened Augustin, which only his mother ever used. He was a stocky, calm, unhurried boy who liked things done properly, and who found, at about the age of nine, that very few things on the Drowned Coast were done properly. The boats were tied badly, the nets were mended in a hurry, the drowned harbour's canals were full of rubbish, and the great sea temple, which a girl had danced dry on the day of the Fall and which the whole Coast loved, was filthy."], ["p", "He began sweeping it at twelve, uninvited. The temple's old keeper, a priest who had survived the Fall on its roof and never quite recovered, let him: he was quiet, he was thorough, and he did not ask questions. By twenty Gus was doing all the keeping and the old priest none of it. In the sixtieth summer the priest died, and the Council of Captains, which has no priests and does not want any, asked Gus to keep the temple, and he took up its broom, and has kept it for forty years, against the sea, the salt and everyone who walks in."], ["p", "The temple is the great hall of the Drowned Harbour: pale pillars, sea-glass windows, its floor just above the water at high tide, and the tide never far away. Gus keeps it as clean as a temple has ever been kept. The floor is always wet, because he holds that a clean floor is a wet one, and he has put up signs. People slide. That, he will tell you, is their own business. He is a stocky, calm old keeper in grey-blue robes and a broad lacquered hat, with a sweeping staff hung with paper streamers and a bronze urn of glowing temple water at his side, which he throws, when he has to."], ["p", "He is the nearest thing the Coast folk have to a priest, which he denies. People come to him with their troubles because he is in the temple, and he listens, and sweeps, and says something sensible, and they go away feeling better and slightly damp. He holds no faith but the Tide, and the Tide, as he explains it, means keep your floor clean and your boat tied and do not expect the sea to be grateful. He has never once asked the sea for anything."], ["p", "[Marin](heroes/marin), the Tide Dancer, is his oldest friend: she danced the water out of his temple on the day of the Fall, before he was born, and he has kept her floor wet ever since. They have tea on the temple steps every morning, and she dances the noon tide on his clean floor, and he does not mind the footprints, from her. He comes to the Ring when [Big Yoko](heroes/big-yoko) has thrown his salt about again. Yoko throws salt before every bout, to keep the circle clean; it ends up all over the Ring's floor; Gus has asked him to stop, many times, and sweeps it up. Each thinks the other is very patient, and they are friends. Gus reads Yoko his letters, and has never told anyone the Mountain cannot read."], ["p", "In the hundredth summer the temple is busier than it has been since the Fall: the Climb's champions come through the Drowned Harbour on their way to the Ring, and stop at the temple to ask for luck, and Gus, who does not give luck, gives them a towel. He has put up a new sign: CLIMBERS, MIND THE FLOOR. Nobody has ever asked him what he thinks is at the top of the Tower. He thinks it needs a sweep."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Coast folk of Saltreach, born after the Fall: sixty-five, stocky and calm, with a heavy-lidded face and a short grey beard under a broad flat lacquered hat; grey-blue robes with a pale crossed collar and a dark sash; the sweeping staff with its straw head and paper streamers in the left hand, and the bronze urn of glowing aqua water at his side."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Slow, mild and unsurprised, a voice that has said \"mind the floor\" ten thousand times. He states rules as facts. He never argues; he repeats himself until the other person gives up."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Mind the floor. I did warn you.\"", "**Picked:** \"Wipe your feet. Then we'll begin.\"", "**Winning:** \"Swept up.\"", "**Winning:** \"Clean. As it should be.\"", "**Losing:** \"Slipped. Happens to everyone.\"", "**Losing:** \"I'll mop that up later.\"", "**Meeting Big Yoko:** \"Salt again? I've only just finished.\"", "**Meeting Marin:** \"Floor's wet, dancer. Just how you like it.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["He knows about the door from the Gaol of Oaths into the temple's undercroft, and he knows the Uncrowned came through it, because he found her wet footprints on his floor that morning and mopped them up before the Chainwarden came. He has not decided why he did it.", "The temple water in his urn glows because it comes from the temple's old well, which goes down below the sea, and nobody knows where. He has never drunk it.", "He does not like the sea. He has kept its temple for forty years and has never once been out in a boat."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "He has been up to the Climb's first floor and seen the sea temple there as it was on the morning of the Fall: filthy, flooding, and full of frightened people. He would like to go back with his broom."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Big Yoko](heroes/big-yoko)**, friend: Yoko throws the salt and Gus sweeps it up, and both think the other is very patient.", "**[Marin](heroes/marin)**, friend: she danced the water out of his temple, and he has kept her floor wet ever since."]]]}, "heroes/hank": {"title": "Hank, the Moss Hewer", "sub": "The Greenwood Moot · [Wood folk](peoples/wood) · from [the Walking Wood](realms/wood)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Hank was born at the edge of the Still Wood twenty-two years before the Fall, in a woodcutters' village called Holloway that no longer exists, the fourth son of a hewer. He was big from the start and quiet from the start, and he learned the axe at eight, the saw at ten, and the rule of the Wood folk woodcutters at the same time: a felled tree must be paid back with a planted one. He has kept it every day of his life. By twenty he was the best hewer on the Wood's edge, with a braided beard already halfway down his chest, and he had planted, by his own count, two thousand trees."], ["p", "He was twenty-two and felling a beech on the day of the Fall. The beech, half cut through, pulled up its roots and walked away from him, with his axe still in it. Then the whole Wood walked. Holloway was in its path. Hank stood in the middle of the village with a borrowed axe and asked the trees, one after another, to leave: he cut the ones that would not. He saved the village that day, and lost it that winter, when the Wood came back the other way in the night. His father died under an oak. Hank buried him and planted the oak's acorn on the grave."], ["p", "Holloway's survivors built a new village on skids, to follow the Wood, and named it Hankshold, to his great embarrassment; it stands now at the Wood's eastern edge, wherever that is this year, with the other edge villages. They have no champion of their own. Hank is their champion. When the Walking Wood walks into a village, someone has to ask it to leave, and Hank asks with an axe."], ["p", "He swore the Moot's oath in the first year after the Fall, because he thought the villages should have a voice in it, and within the year he grew horns: broad branching antlers of bone, like a stag's. He found them inconvenient. They caught in branches and doorways and made him three feet taller than any roof in Hankshold. The next spring, when the sworn shed their antlers and plant the tips, he did not wait for them to fall; he sawed them off, flat, at every tip, and planted the tips. He has done it every spring since. They grow back broader every year. The Moot says it is the only thing he plants willingly, which is unfair, and also the only joke the Moot has ever made."], ["p", "He is the Moot's woodcutter: huge and quiet, in a moss-green tunic and a fur mantle, with a braided beard and a great one-bit axe whose edge glows green from a working the Briarkin put on it in exchange for a winter's firewood. He grows trees too, fast, because a felled tree must be paid back: in the Ring he plants pines that are cover while they stand and fall where he chops them. [Fenna](heroes/fenna) the Grove Warden calls him a butcher and he calls her a gardener; they have argued about every tree he has felled for twenty years, and shared a fire every winter for the same twenty, at the clearing where his hut and her garden meet. He hunts with the Huntmaster who tracks what lives under the trees while Hank deals with the trees, and drinks with [Bo](heroes/bo), the Ramherd, with whom he has shared a fire, a flock and a great many silences."], ["p", "In year 47 the Underforge's timber buyers and the Moot came close to war over a stretch of the Wood's edge on the old Harrow road, and the quarrel was settled in the Ring: Hank against an Underforge champion. Hank won, and the edge was the Wood's. The Underforge still buys the timber; it pays Hank's price now, which includes a planted tree for every felled one, planted by the buyer."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Wood folk of the Wood's edge, sworn to the Moot, and Noon-touched: twenty-two at the Fall, he looks forty-two. Huge and quiet, with ruddy skin, moss-green eyes and a braided brown beard. Out of his hair, behind the leather brow band, rise the antlers of the sworn: broad and branching, sweeping out and back, and sawn flat at every tip, the pale cut wood showing, because he cuts them back each spring and plants the tips. A moss-green tunic under a fur mantle, and the one-bit axe held low with its edge aglow green. He is the Horned now, and stands apart from every other workman in the Ring."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Deep, slow and few-worded. He talks to trees more than to people and says \"mind your heads\" more than anything else. When he is angry he goes quiet; when he is very angry he goes back to work."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Timber. Mind your heads.\"", "**Picked:** \"One to fell, one to plant.\"", "**Winning:** \"Down. I'll plant one for you.\"", "**Winning:** \"Clean cut.\"", "**Losing:** \"Bark's thicker than you'd think.\"", "**Losing:** \"Saw me off, did you? It grows back.\"", "**Meeting Fenna:** \"I'll plant two. Happy?\"", "**Meeting Bo:** \"Fire tonight? Bring the cider.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["He has been up the Climb to the Wood's eleventh floor and cut a piece of a root that went up into the Tower's wall. It bled sap that glowed. He gave it to Fenna and did not tell her he had cut his hand on it, and that the cut has not healed.", "The acorn he planted on his father's grave grew into an oak that the Wood took when it walked. He has looked for it for a hundred years.", "He can read, and writes poems about trees, which nobody has seen."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "For the villages at the Wood's edge to stop being walked over. If the Tower is why the Wood walks, he would like to have a word with it, with the axe."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Fenna](heroes/fenna)**, rival: she grows them, he fells them. They argue every day and share a fire every winter.", "**[Bo](heroes/bo)**, friend: they have shared a fire, a flock and a great many silences."]]]}, "heroes/isolde": {"title": "Isolde, the Winter Warden", "sub": "The Hourkeepers · [Northerners](peoples/north), lake-born · from [Frostfall](realms/frostfall)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "At the top of the Dial, where the stars turn round the pole, the cold keeps time still, and Isolde guards it. She was born twenty-eight years before the Fall in a shore village of Frostfall Lake, to one of the lake-born families: the old Northern lineage that has kept the lake's ice since the first star-watchers, frost-pale from generations of the cold, white-haired from birth. The lake-born have always given the Dial its **Wardens of the Winter**, the Northern warriors who hold the ice, and every generation one of them is the Winter Warden, who keeps the cold itself: the cold that keeps the frozen library dry and still, so that nothing written in it fades."], ["p", "Isolde was chosen at twelve, because she could walk on ice no one else could stand on, and could make water freeze by holding it. She was trained by the old Warden on the lake for fifteen years, in the long nights, with an ice spear and a crown of ice spikes that grows on the Warden's head and is not worn but kept, and at twenty-seven she was the Winter Warden. A year later the sun fell, and the winter that came after it did not end until the next summer, and the herds starved on the moors, and the library under the ice began to drip for the first time in its history, because the slipping hours had confused the cold. Isolde stood on the lake for that whole winter and the summer after, alone, holding the cold still with her spear in the ice, until it remembered how to keep. The Northerners call it the Long Hold. The library has not dripped since."], ["p", "She is the Winter Warden, in white and frost-blue, with a crown of ice spikes fanned behind her head and a long ice spear, and the Hourkeepers' library at Frostfall lies in ice under her watch, where nothing written can fade. Her hits stack the cold on her enemies until they freeze. The lake-born age slowly, and the Noon-touched slower, and the Warden's cold has crept into her skin over seventy years until it is nearly white with a blue undertone; she looks forty-eight and has not felt warm since the Long Hold."], ["p", "She lives on the lake. The Warden's house is a round hut of black stone on an island at the lake's centre, reached over the ice in winter and by boat in the brief summer, with a door that faces the library's entrance across the water. She goes down into the library every night and walks its shelves with a lamp, touching the ice of the walls to feel whether the cold is holding, and the Readers working late know the sound of her spear on the floor. She trains the Wardens of the Winter on the ice at dawn, the lake-born young who will hold it after her, and has chosen none of them to be the next Winter Warden yet, which worries the lake villages."], ["p", "Except once. In year 40, the smith of the Underforge, [Brannoc](heroes/brannoc), came north to Frostfall Lake with a forge in his chest, following her, fire to her frost: they had met at the Ring the summer before and he had decided, in his way, that she was the one person on the Dial who could stand his heat. He set up his forge on the lake's shore, and the ice cracked, and the library under it began to drip. Isolde froze his forge solid to send him home. It took him a year to light it again. It was the kindest thing she knew how to do: she could not have him there, and she could not have asked him to leave, so she made him. Neither of them has married."], ["p", "The aurora dancer, who dances the northern light, is her younger sister: the warden of the cold and the dancer of its light, daughters of the same frozen lake. They are close in the quiet Northern way, which means they say little and are always within reach. Isolde fights to keep the cold, and the past, as they are."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "A Northerner of the lake-born families, Noon-touched: twenty-eight at the Fall, she looks forty-eight, frost-pale with a blue undertone and white hair, as her lineage is and as the Warden's cold has made her more so. White and frost-blue robes in a bell to the floor, a frost fern pattern at the hem, a crown of ice spikes fanned behind her head, and the long ice spear in the left hand."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Slow, cool and quiet, with long pauses; she never says more than she needs to. She speaks to fire as if it were a child. Her kindness sounds like a reprimand."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Slower. Slower. There.\"", "**Picked:** \"Cold keeps. Let it keep you.\"", "**Winning:** \"Frozen. Kept.\"", "**Winning:** \"Be still. The winter is patient.\"", "**Losing:** \"A thaw. It won't last.\"", "**Losing:** \"The lake will freeze again.\"", "**Meeting Brannoc:** \"You're dripping on my ice, smith.\"", "**Meeting the aurora dancer:** \"Dance, sister. I'll keep the cold.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["During the Long Hold, the cold spoke to her. It said the hours were slipping because something had stopped them at the Tower, and that if the Tower's hour were set going again, the cold would have to let go of everything it keeps: the library, the lake, and her.", "She keeps the forge she froze. It is still on the lake's shore under a dome of ice, and on the coldest nights, through the ice, it glows.", "She has never been warm since the Long Hold. Brannoc was the only time."]], ["h", 2, "What she wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To keep the hours as they are until the Hourkeepers know why they slipped. If the sun is hung again, the cold will have to let go, and she is not sure she can."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Brannoc](heroes/brannoc)**, old flame: fire followed frost north once. She froze his forge solid to send him home, and neither has married."]]]}, "heroes/kaia": {"title": "Kaia, the Tempest Caller", "sub": "The Windward · [Coast folk](peoples/coast) · from [the Drowned Coast](realms/coast)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Kaia was born on the Storm Spire in the seventy-second summer after the Fall, during a storm, which the storm-callers of the Spires consider the only proper way to be born. Her mother was a storm-caller and her father a lightning-rod smith from Saltreach, and she grew up in the storm-callers' tower at the top of the Spire, a thousand feet above the sea, in rooms full of copper rods and weather-glasses, with the sky outside the windows doing whatever it liked. It always had. Kaia was born into a world whose weather had had no master for seventy-two years, and she never knew another."], ["p", "The storm-callers of the Spires are an old order, older than the Fall, and before the Fall their work was small: a wind for a becalmed ship, rain for a dry field, a storm turned away from the harbour. The weather had a master then, the sun, and the seasons kept their appointments, and the storm-callers only nudged. Since the Fall the storms come when they like, and the storm-callers have become the Coast's defence: they call down the lightning before it can fall on the town, and turn the storms that come off the Sands out to sea. Kaia was the best of her generation by fifteen and the least obedient by sixteen. She did not want to turn storms away. She wanted to make them come."], ["p", "She means to be the sky's master, since nobody else is. She is a storm caller of the Spires, with a tall staff that catches lightning and plants it in the ground as rods, and her lightning goes where she puts it, in branching trees of hard blue-white light under flat dark cloud. She bends the storms to the Windward's ships, and sells rain to the Wood when the Wood pays, and has sold storms to people the Council of Captains pretends not to know about. She fights in the Ring because a storm needs somewhere to land. Her first duel there, at nineteen, emptied the western stands with a stray bolt, and is the reason the Truce hired a hedge witch to salt the Ring's edge."], ["p", "[Barry Cloudsley](heroes/barry-cloudsley), the Windward's augur, forecasts the weather from the Weather Spire; Kaia changes it, mostly to prove him wrong. It began in year 88, when Barry forecast a clear day for the Spires' Kite Day and Kaia, sixteen and furious about something else, called a thunderstorm on it at noon in front of the whole coast. Barry said she had broken the forecast. Kaia said she had improved it. They have argued in public for twelve years since, on the Weather Spire's balcony, in the Council of Captains and in the Ring, and the Coast follows it like a play. They are the two best weather-workers on the Dial and they have never once agreed on anything. Each is, in private, the other's greatest admirer."], ["p", "She lives, still, in the storm-callers' tower at the top of the Storm Spire, in a round room with nine windows and a copper rod through the roof that she sleeps beside on purpose. The order elected her its first caller at twenty-five, over the heads of a dozen elders, because nobody else could hold the great storm of the ninety-seventh summer off the Lantern Coast; she held it for a day and a night, alone on the Spire's top with the rod in her hands, and when it broke she walked down nine hundred steps and slept for two days. The Coast forgave her a great deal for that night, and she has spent most of it since."], ["p", "And the aurora dancer, the dancer of the northern light at Frostfall: lightning and the aurora have never shared a sky politely. When Kaia's storms go north they foul the aurora dancer's curtains, and when the aurora dancer's curtains come south the Spires get green flashes at sea that sailors take for omens. They met in the Ring once and the stands talked about the colours for a year."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Coast folk of the Spires, born after the Fall: twenty-eight, lean and quick, with sea-tanned skin. Her silver hair is salt-bleached and bound up in a knot at the back of her head, one sea-green lock falling by her cheek where she dyes it with kelp. Round both forearms, bared by sleeves rolled to the elbow, run the tide lines of the nine great floods she has lived through, and sea-glass hangs in her ears. She wears a knee-length storm coat of rain-blue oilskin, open over a dark tunic, with a high collar and a short cape lined in storm blue, the storm clouds on her shoulders and a gold bolt on her chest; dark leggings and sea boots below. The hooked staff with a spark caught in its hook is in her left hand. She reads as a storm caller of the Spires, not a lady of a court, and nobody takes her for Isolde any more."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Sharp, bright and fast, with a laugh like a crack of thunder; she talks over people, especially Barry. She swears in the Spires' whistle-talk, which is mostly the sound of wind."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Stand by the rod. It's safer. For me.\"", "**Picked:** \"Forecast: lightning. Mine.\"", "**Winning:** \"Struck.\"", "**Winning:** \"Tell Barry he was wrong. Again.\"", "**Losing:** \"Clear skies. For now.\"", "**Losing:** \"Every storm blows over. Then it comes back.\"", "**Meeting Barry Cloudsley:** \"What's the forecast, old man? Wrong?\"", "**Meeting the aurora dancer:** \"Pretty lights. Shame about the weather.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["She has tried to call a storm over the Tower, three times, to see what the sky does there. All three times the storm went round. She thinks the Tower is holding something up, or back.", "The storms Barry thinks someone in the Sands is calling: some of them she called, for pay, for a house of the Dune Throne. She did not know whom they would hit. One hit the Spires.", "She keeps every one of Barry's forecasts, in a box, in order."]], ["h", 2, "What she wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To stand on top of it in a storm and call the lightning down onto the Noon Seat, and see what lights up. She believes the sky can be mastered, and that the master's seat is up there."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Barry Cloudsley](heroes/barry-cloudsley)**, rival: he forecasts the weather and she changes it to prove him wrong; twelve years of public argument."]]]}, "heroes/lyra": {"title": "Lyra, the Star Archer", "sub": "The Hourkeepers · [Northerners](peoples/north) · from [Frostfall](realms/frostfall)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "The stars still keep time, even with the sun gone wrong. The sun's shadow stopped at the Fall, and the hours slip, and the days run long and short; but the stars over Frostfall still turn round the Nail every night exactly as they did for the first star-watchers twelve hundred years ago, and the Hourkeepers set their clocks by them. Lyra reads them."], ["p", "She was born in the observatory itself, in the seventy-eighth summer after the Fall, in a room under the dome where her mother, a Reader of the night sky, had been charting a comet and refused to stop. She grew up on the observatory's roof terraces at night, wrapped in reindeer furs, with the Great Lens behind her and the whole sky in front, learning the constellations the way other children learn the faces of their family. She could name every star the Hourkeepers had charted by eight, and found her first uncharted one at ten, a faint red star low over the ice coast that the library now calls Lyra's Lamp."], ["p", "She learned the bow on the moors from the Wardens of the Winter, because the observatory's Readers go out on the ice at night to measure and the ice has wolves. She was good at it. She was better at it than at anything else, except the stars, and at fourteen she found that the two were one thing: an arrow loosed at a star-point she had charted drew a line of light behind it, as a star's motion draws a line on a long exposure of the Lens, and where two lines met, they made a figure, and the figure held. Her arrows draw constellations between them as they fly: a net, a bow, a stag, gold lines from star to star that snap and fall as shooting stars. She is the Hourkeepers' archer, a night hunter in a starry violet cloak with a gold crescent bow, and she hits harder the farther away you stand, because a star is always far."], ["p", "She climbed the Tower for the first time in year 98, with a party of Hourkeepers sent to measure, and went up through the Sea's floors to its crown with her bow strung and her eyes on the sky. The Tower's floors have skies of their own, and the stars in them, she found, are the stars of the hour each floor holds: the Sea's sky is the sky of the morning after the Fall, with the old sun's light still fading from it. She charted it. It is the last chart of the old sky anyone has made, and the library keeps it beside the Noon Folio."], ["p", "She charts the northern sky with the aurora dancer, the dancer of the northern light, from the observatory: partners at the observatory, one who charts the stars and the other the lights between them. On nights the stars are hidden, the aurora dancer dances the aurora and Lyra reads the hour from its curtains, and they have kept the Hourkeepers' night record together for six years without missing an hour."], ["p", "Her stars are the one light [Nyx](heroes/nyx), the Court of Dusk's knife, cannot step out of: a shadow needs light that comes from somewhere, and starlight comes from everywhere at once. They first met on the night roads in year 95, when Nyx was hunting a Hourkeeper surveyor and Lyra was walking home; Lyra lit the sky, and the surveyor walked home. They have duelled in the dark more often than either has told anyone, and Lyra has won more than half, and has never once tried to finish it. And the mirage archer of the Sands calls himself the better archer, and she lets him: two archers who have never shot at the same target, and argue about it at every Truce dinner. She lets him because it is funnier, and because it is not true."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "A Northerner of the observatory, born after the Fall: twenty-two, with deep brown skin and long dark hair, a starry violet cloak and tunic, a gold constellation halo behind her head, and the gold crescent bow across her back. Her hair has not gone white yet; Northern hair goes white by thirty, and she has eight years."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Bright, clear and a little dreamy, with the habit of looking past whoever she is talking to, at the sky. She names things by stars. She is very kind and very hard to make angry."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Run. The sky is very big, and I can see all of it.\"", "**Picked:** \"Clear night. Good shooting.\"", "**Winning:** \"Charted.\"", "**Winning:** \"You're a constellation now. A small one.\"", "**Losing:** \"Clouds. They pass.\"", "**Losing:** \"The stars are still there. So am I.\"", "**Meeting Nyx:** \"There you are. I can always see you, you know.\"", "**Meeting the mirage archer:** \"You're the better archer, the mirage archer. Of course.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["The red star she found at ten, Lyra's Lamp, is moving. It moves toward the Tower, a little every year. She believes it is something coming.", "She could finish any duel with Nyx. She has never wanted to. She thinks Nyx is the loneliest person on the Dial.", "The stars over the Tower are wrong: in the gap above the Tear, there are stars that are on no chart, and they are arranged in a figure she does not know."]], ["h", 2, "What she wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To stand on the Noon Seat at night and chart the stars above the Tear: the ones on no chart, in the figure she does not know. She thinks they will tell her what came through."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Nyx](heroes/nyx)**, rival: the shadow and the star archer; Lyra's stars are the one light Nyx cannot step out of."]]]}, "heroes/mabel": {"title": "Mabel, the Deepdelver", "sub": "The Underforge · [Mole-folk](peoples/mole) · from [the Deep](realms/deep)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Mabel was born in the warren of the Deepest, the lowest of the mole-folk's towns under the southern hills, twenty years before the Fall by the Hourkeepers' count and \"in the year of the warm seam\" by her own people's. The Deepest is a warren of round rooms joined by tunnels exactly as wide as a mole-folk's shoulders, in rock that is warm and still and dark, and Mabel grew up in the dark as her people always had: digging, listening to the rock, learning the seam-songs from her mother, who learned them from hers. She was stout, dark-furred and curious, and she dug faster than any girl in the warren."], ["p", "She was twenty and at the face of a new seam on the day of the Fall. She heard the rock sing, a high long note she had never heard before, and then she saw a light in the rock: a vein of crystal she had uncovered that morning, dull and grey, filling from inside with pale rose light, as if something were pouring into it from above. The light ran along the vein faster than she could follow, into the next crystal and the next, through the whole of the Deep, until every crystal in the halls glowed. The mole-folk call it **the Shining**. Mabel saw it first."], ["p", "Her people are divided about it still. The old say it ruined the dark, which they had loved for nine hundred years, and that a light that comes from above is not to be trusted. Mabel's generation says the light came to them, through their rock, to their crystals, and that it belongs to the miners who dug down to it. Mabel says so loudest. She is a miner of the deepest seams, and a lamp-keeper: she keeps the **Lamp Road** open, the mole-folk's great tunnel from the Deep's mouth down to the crystal halls, and she has fitted every crystal along it with a bronze collar so that it can be carried to light another tunnel. She wears a bronze helm topped with a glowing amber crystal lantern of her own, cut from the seam where she saw the Shining."], ["p", "The Noonsworn want every drop of the old light carried up to the sky, and [Opaline](heroes/opaline), the Lightbreaker, goes down the Prism Stair every day and breaks it out of the crystals and sends it to Aubade. Mabel has told her that the light belongs to the miners. She has told her with her claws, which are as long as daggers and tipped in gold, in the Ring, three times. The Deep is still arguing. Mabel rather likes Opaline, which makes it worse."], ["p", "She digs with the Hallbreaker, the Underforge's sapper, a younger mole-folk who uses powder instead of claws, which Mabel considers cheating and very efficient: they have opened more fallen halls together than any pair in the Deep. And she sorts glass with the Obsidian Herald, the Herald of the Caldera: Mabel digs the black glass out of the Caldera's cooled edges, where the sun's fire turns to obsidian, and the Obsidian Herald cuts it, and the two of them run the trade in black glass that pays for half the Underforge's iron."], ["p", "In the Ring she goes under the floor and comes up under you. She does not like the open, or the light at the Ring, which hurts her small eyes; she fights fast and leaves."], ["p", "Since the Climb opened she has been up to the Deep's chapter, the far halls on the night their crystals went out, and walked them in the dark without a lamp, as her people did before the Shining, and found it, she says, restful. The Echo-Hermit there clicks his tongue to find his way, a trick he learned from a mole-folk Old Mother, and Mabel taught him two more seam-songs before she came down."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Mole-folk of the Deepest, and Noon-touched (one of the few: most of her people's elders were too deep to be touched by anything): twenty at the Fall, she looks forty in mole-folk years. Stout and dark-furred, with a long muzzle and a small rose nose, calm black eyes, a studded leather cuirass, a bronze helm with a gold band and brass goggles pushed up, a glowing amber crystal lantern on top, a slung pick, and huge gold-tipped claws."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Brisk, friendly and literal, with the mole-folk's round vowels and a habit of sniffing before she answers. She says exactly what she means and is puzzled when others do not. She hums seam-songs when she digs."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Mind the hole. Well. Mind the next one.\"", "**Picked:** \"Down we go.\"", "**Winning:** \"Dug in.\"", "**Winning:** \"Up from under. Hello.\"", "**Losing:** \"Too bright up here. I'm going down.\"", "**Losing:** \"Cave-in. Happens.\"", "**Meeting Opaline:** \"That's our light, glass-girl. Put it back.\"", "**Meeting the Hallbreaker:** \"Claws first, powder second. Agreed?\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["The crystal in her lantern still holds the first light of the Shining. On some nights it sings the high note she heard in the rock. She thinks it is calling the rest of the light home, and she does not know where home is.", "She has dug deeper than any mole-folk has ever dug, below the Deepest, and found a tunnel going down that was dug from below. She filled it in. She has told only her Old Mother.", "She agrees with Opaline more than she lets on. The light does not belong to anyone."]], ["h", 2, "What she wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To see the light from above, once, where it came from; and then to bring it home to the Deep, where it is safe."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Opaline](heroes/opaline)**, rival: Opaline takes the Deep's light up to Aubade; Mabel wants it left for the miners who dug to it."]]]}, "heroes/madame-cleaver": {"title": "Madame Cleaver, the Feastmistress", "sub": "The Truce of the Ring · [Dune folk](peoples/dune) · from [the Field of Banners](realms/banners)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Madame Cleaver was born on a Brass Road caravan in the Sands, twenty-five years before the Fall, the daughter of the caravan's cook. Her mother cooked for forty camel-drivers on one fire every night for thirty years, and taught her daughter two things: everyone eats from the same pot, and nobody argues with the cook. The cleaver was her mother's: a heavy Kilnborn blade bought in the Deep at the far end of the road, with a hole near its spine so it could hang from the cart."], ["p", "When the sun fell she was twenty-five and cooking for a Dune Throne caravan that had come north to sell glass at the midsummer market. The caravan was on the Banner Road when the shadow stopped. In the chaos of the first months it was pressed into the service of King Orlan's crimson banner, along with its camels and its cook, and she spent the first winter of the War of the Banners feeding the crimson army in the orchards of Harrow."], ["p", "The second winter was the Winter of Mud. Neither banner could move; both were starving. The azure camped across the orchard, close enough to smell the crimson's cooking. One night a starving azure boy crept over to steal from her pot, and she caught him by the ear, and fed him. The next night there were six. By the end of the month she was feeding both armies from one fire in the ruined orchard of Harrow, under a flag of white linen she had hung on her cauldron, and the soldiers of both banners ate side by side and did not fight within sight of it. The officers of both sides tried to stop her. She told them to sit down and eat, and they did."], ["p", "When the war ended and the Truce was sworn at the Tower's foot, the founders added a clause they had learned in her orchard: whoever eats at the Truce Table may not strike in anger outside the Ring. Her cauldron's stand became the Table's first leg. She has kept the Table ever since. Every duel in the Ring ends there, winner and loser side by side, and she fights to get them there: a champion who will not come to the Table is brought, and she does the bringing. She walked away from the war with her cleaver, her cauldron, and the certainty that everyone fights better fed. She is Noon-touched, twenty-five at the Fall, and looks forty-five, broad and proud, with a voice that carries across a battlefield because it has."], ["p", "Her cleaver went back to the Deep for a new edge in year 30, to the great forge, where [Brannoc](heroes/brannoc) made it again: same blade, same hole, an edge that glows like an ember when she is angry. A promise came back with it, which neither of them kept. They still eat at the same table every midsummer, at opposite ends. And she has fed [Big Yoko](heroes/big-yoko) since he was a boy in the straw-rope circle: nobody else on the Dial owns a pot big enough, and nobody else would dare tell the Mountain to finish his greens."], ["p", "The Truce Table is hers in every way that matters. She decides the seating: rivals side by side, sworn enemies across from each other, so they must pass the bread. She cooks the truce-pot herself on the night of every great duel, and she has a rule that nobody leaves until their bowl is empty, which has kept the Countess of Gloaming and the Dawn Paladin at the same table until morning more than once. The Ringside's cooks are her apprentices, every one, and the stew in every kitchen round the crater is hers by descent. She has fed every champion of the Ring for a hundred years, and she could tell you what each of them likes, and what each of them is afraid of, which is usually the same thing."], ["p", "Her recipes came from nobody. Her temper comes from the fact that champions never say thank you."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Dune folk of the caravans, born on the Brass Road: broad and strong, with a round proud face, green eyes (her father's, a caravan guard of the Coast) and a braid at her shoulder. A crimson headwrap in the Sands fashion, mail shoulders, a crimson jerkin with a bandolier of small knives, and the great dark cleaver with its glowing ember edge. The headwrap stays."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Loud, warm and absolutely certain, a voice made for calling forty camel-drivers to supper. She gives orders as if they were invitations and invitations as if they were orders. She swears in the desert tongue, so that only the Dune folk blush."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Sit. Eat. Then you can hit each other.\"", "**Picked:** \"Who's hungry? Good. Get hungrier.\"", "**Winning:** \"Plate's clean. Next.\"", "**Winning:** \"Now you sit at my table. Elbows off.\"", "**Losing:** \"Burnt. Happens to the best pots.\"", "**Losing:** \"Back to the kitchen. I'll be out again.\"", "**Meeting Big Yoko:** \"Did you eat? You didn't eat. Sit.\"", "**Meeting Brannoc:** \"Still not sharp enough, smith. Neither are you.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["The starving azure boy she caught stealing from her pot on the first night of the Winter of Mud was the drummer of the azure, the boy whose drum still beats on the Climb's forty-eighth floor. She has climbed to the forty-first and turned back. She cannot face the rest.", "The promise she and Brannoc did not keep was to open an inn together at the Waymeet when the war was really over. She still has the deed.", "She has never once eaten at the Truce Table herself."]], ["h", 2, "What she wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To feed the War chapter's two armies at one fire, once, and see if they stop. The Camp-Mother on its floors learned the trick from her, and it has not worked yet."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Big Yoko](heroes/big-yoko)**, friend: she has fed the Mountain since he was a boy; nobody else owns a pot big enough.", "**[Brannoc](heroes/brannoc)**, old flame: her cleaver came from his forge, and so did a promise neither of them kept."]]]}, "heroes/marin": {"title": "Marin, the Tide Dancer", "sub": "The Windward · [Coast folk](peoples/coast) · from [the Drowned Coast](realms/coast)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Marin was born in Sallowmouth, the great port of the Drowned Coast, twelve years before the Fall, the daughter of a harbour pilot and a fishwife, and at seven she was given to the sea temple as a dancer. The Tide Dancers of the sea temple were an old order: girls and boys who danced the turning of the tide on the temple floor at every high water and every low, so that the harbour would remember to breathe, the Coast folk said. It was not a working, exactly. It was a courtesy to the sea. Marin was the best of them by ten, barefoot on the cold stone in a dress of sea-glass beads, with ribbons of blue silk that she swung like water."], ["p", "She was twelve and dancing the noon tide in the temple on the day of the Fall. The sun stopped over the Tower; the light changed; the sea began to come in, not as a wave but as a rising, steady and enormous, over the quays, the fish market, the streets, the first floors of the houses. The priests fled up the tower. The other dancers fled. Marin did not, because nobody had told her to stop, and because the tide was turning and she was in the middle of the turn. She danced. And the water that came in at the temple's great doors slowed, and curled, and turned with her ribbons, and went back out."], ["p", "She danced for eleven hours, the old keeper said afterwards. The sea came in over the whole of Sallowmouth that afternoon and drowned half the city, and stayed; but the floor of the sea temple was dry at nightfall, and the people who had climbed onto its roof came down into it alive. The Coast folk call it the **Dry Floor**, and it is why the temple still stands in the Drowned Harbour with its floor just above the water. The ribbons she danced with had turned, in her hands, into ribbons of water: thick, smooth bands of clear blue water with a white foam crest. They have never turned back."], ["p", "She has danced the tide ever since. It turns every few seconds in her hands now: high, it pushes; low, it pulls. She is the Windward's tide dancer, and the Drowned Harbour's, and she keeps the harbour lights with them and the boats off the drowned streets. She holds the gate of the Climb's Sea, where the Tower holds Sallowmouth on the morning the water came, and in the Tower, as on that day, she decides when the tide goes out."], ["p", "[Gus](heroes/gus) keeps her temple, and has kept it clean for forty years against the sea, the salt and everyone who walks in: she danced the water out of it, and he has kept her floor wet ever since, because a clean floor, he says, is a wet one. They have tea on the temple steps every morning. The ferryman of the dead poles his rivers of pale light down to the sea, and her tide carries them out; she asks him nothing about what they carry, and he is grateful. And the Chainwarden of the Gaol of Oaths under the drowned harbour wall, blames Marin's tide for every flood of her lower cells and every escape. Marin says the tide turns itself. The Chainwarden says that is exactly what someone who turned it would say. They have argued on the temple steps for twenty years, while Gus sweeps round them."], ["p", "She is quiet and warm and a little distant, as people are who have spent their lives listening to something nobody else can hear. She has a coral flower in her hair, from the reef off the Spires, that a boy gave her before the Fall. He drowned that afternoon."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Coast folk of Sallowmouth, Noon-touched: twelve at the Fall, she looks thirty-two. Lithe and barefoot (her page always said so), with teal-dyed hair and a coral flower in it, a wave-blue wrap on the shoulders, a skirt of tiered blue, and sashes of water sweeping round her."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Soft and rhythmic, rising and falling as if she were counting a tide; she speaks in short phrases with pauses between. She is patient with everyone except people who track water into the temple."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"The sea comes in. The sea goes out. You don't.\"", "**Picked:** \"Listen. It's turning.\"", "**Winning:** \"Washed out.\"", "**Winning:** \"The tide decides. It decided.\"", "**Losing:** \"Low water. It comes back.\"", "**Losing:** \"Even the sea gets tired.\"", "**Meeting the Chainwarden:** \"The tide doesn't keep prisoners, the Chainwarden. Neither do I.\"", "**Meeting Gus:** \"Mind the floor, Gus. I'll dance round it.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["She did not choose to keep dancing on the day of the Fall. She could not stop. The tide would not let her. She has been afraid of it ever since, and loves it.", "When she dances at the Climb's gate, the drowned dockers of the first floor stop and watch. One of them is the boy who gave her the coral flower.", "She knows the door the Uncrowned used to leave the Gaol: it opens into the temple's undercroft, under her dry floor. She found it the morning after, wet. She has told nobody but Gus."]], ["h", 2, "What she wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To dance the tide out of the Drowned Harbour, as she danced it out of the temple; and if the Tower holds Sallowmouth as it was, to bring its drowned home."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Gus](heroes/gus)**, friend: she danced the water out of his temple, and he has kept her floor wet ever since."]]]}, "heroes/mortis": {"title": "Mortis, the Ossuary Lord", "sub": "The Court of Dusk · [the Long Dead](peoples/longdead) · from [Gloaming](realms/gloaming)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Three hundred years before the Fall, in a tall narrow house in the scholars' ring of Gloaming, a man called Aurel Vane sat up late over a question. He was a scholar of the dead: he had spent forty years in the ossuaries under the city with a lamp and a notebook, among the halls of bone the Ossuary Pact had sealed a century and a half before, asking the Long Dead why they had got up and the others had not. He had filled sixty notebooks. On the night in question he had found, he believed, the answer, and he dipped his pen to write it down, and his heart stopped."], ["p", "Three days later, in the family crypt under the house, he got up. His Reason, the linen strip the Long Dead keep inside the ribs, says only: \"to finish the sentence.\" He has never told anyone what the sentence was. He gave up his living name, which is cut on a stone in the family crypt that he does not visit, and took an ossuary name, Mortis, and went down into the halls of bone to work."], ["p", "He found that he was very good at it. Every dead thing that falls near him leaves its bones, and every bone, he discovered, is his to raise: not as a person, as the Long Dead rise, but as a thing, a wall, a spire, a soldier, a place to stand. Within a century the Long Dead of the ossuaries had made him their lord, because he understood them better than they understood themselves, and because he was the only one of them who could make the Restless, the dead who get up without a will, do anything useful. Under Gloaming lie the ossuaries, halls of bone older than the Court of Dusk, stacked skull on skull and lit by teal soulfire, and Mortis is their lord."], ["p", "Since the Fall the dead of the Dial have been restless, and Mortis offers them work. The drowned of the Coast, the soldiers of the Banners, the hungry of the Long Dusk: the dead get up more often now, without a will of their own, and wander, and frighten the living. Mortis gathers them. He gives them walls to stand in, spires to hold up, ranks to march in, a place, which is more than the living gave most of them. He thinks of it as a kindness. The ferryman of the dead one of the Long Dead himself, who takes the dead across to rest, thinks of it as theft, and the two have not spoken civilly in a century. The Saltwarden, the Truce's Saltwarden, lays her salt so that his skeletons cannot reach the Ring's crowd, and he has never forgiven her for making his servants look foolish in front of forty thousand people."], ["p", "He allied himself with the Court of Dusk when Vesper founded it, by the terms of the old Ossuary Pact, as the Lord Below: an ally, not a subject. The Court buries in the Wood's soil the dead it cannot fit under Gloaming, which the Greenwood Moot hates, and Mortis raises them anyway, which the Moot hates more."], ["p", "There is one body that will not stay down. In year 40, after a duel on the Field of Banners, Mortis buried the ashes of [Anka](heroes/anka), the Firebird, in a lead box under the ossuaries, because the dead should stay where they are put and an Ember that rises again offends everything he is. She rose in the box, melted it, and walked out through the Night Temple in the middle of the Naming, leaving burning footprints on the floor. He buried her again in year 66, in iron, under the black water of the Deep, and in year 83, in salt, which nearly worked. She has never stayed. He fights in the Ring for more ground, and to bury her a fourth time. He has a box ready. It is very beautiful."], ["p", "He is not cruel, and he would be hurt to be called so. He is patient, scholarly, dry and courteous, and he thinks the living make far too much fuss about the end of things. \"Rest,\" he says, \"is for those who have somewhere to be.\""], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "One of the Long Dead, three hundred years from his death and long past vellum: a skull face with teal soulfire in the sockets under a dark hood with teal wisps, a long robe in grey-green with carved rib arcs over the chest, and a skull-topped staff in the left hand."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Dry, slow and scholarly, with a faint echo, as if he were speaking from the bottom of a stair. He lectures. He corrects your grammar while he kills you."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Rest is for those who have somewhere to be.\"", "**Picked:** \"Bones, please. Single file.\"", "**Winning:** \"Lie still. You'll find it restful.\"", "**Winning:** \"Another for the wall.\"", "**Losing:** \"A temporary condition. Believe me.\"", "**Losing:** \"I have been dead longer than this.\"", "**Meeting Anka:** \"Ah. The one that won't stay down. I brought a box.\"", "**Meeting the ferryman of the dead:** \"Ferryman. Still giving them away?\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["The sentence he died finishing was the answer to why the dead get up. He has never written it, because if he finishes it his Reason ends and he lies down. Some nights he is tempted.", "He envies Anka. She rises without a Reason. He has studied her ashes, all three times, and found nothing in them but ash.", "The Restless have got up more often every year since the Fall, and he has measured it: the curve matches the Hourkeepers' paling of the light exactly."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "More ground, and more dead to give it to. And, at the top, the Noon Seat kept empty, as the Pact requires; but if there is a body up there, he would like to bury it."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Anka](heroes/anka)**, sworn enemy: he keeps the dead in the ground; she will not stay there. He has buried her three times."]]]}, "heroes/mr-ledger": {"title": "Mr. Ledger, the Tithe-Keeper", "sub": "The Truce of the Ring · [the Long Dead](peoples/longdead) · from [the Ring](realms/tower)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Before he was the Tithe-Keeper, Mr. Ledger was a counting-house clerk in Harrow, the castle town of the old kingdom of Harrowmark, in the days when its crimson kings grew fat on the tolls of the road to the Tower. He was precise, unremarkable and unmarried, and he kept the royal tolls for thirty years without a single error, which made him the most hated man in the kingdom and the most trusted. Sixty years before the Fall, on a winter evening, he sat down to reconcile the year's accounts, and found a discrepancy of one coin."], ["p", "He died at his desk that night, of his heart, with the pen in his hand and the sum unfinished. Three days later, in the crypt under the counting house, he got up. The Long Dead do not choose their Reason; it chooses them. His was the sum. He walked back up the stairs, sat down at his desk, and went on looking for the missing coin."], ["p", "He never found it. Harrowmark's kings learned not to mind a dead clerk; he was cheaper than a living one and he could not be bribed. He kept the kingdom's books through three reigns, and when King Orlan raised the crimson banner after the Fall, Ledger kept the books of the war: every sword, every sack of grain, every life, at what it cost and to whom. When the war ended in the mud and Orlan was buried, the founders of the Truce found that the only complete record of what every realm owed every other was in a dead man's ledger. They offered him the Tally. He accepted on one condition: that no debt in his book would ever be forgiven, only paid. He has kept the Tally since the day the Truce was sworn, in a great red book chained to his wrist so that nobody can borrow it."], ["p", "He is older than he looks, and he looks very old. The realms fear him more than any champion, because a debt in his book does not fade; it waits. His workings are debts too: nothing hurts now, everything comes due. He steps into the Ring when a debtor will not pay any other way, and he has never once lost the paperwork."], ["p", "He keeps strict hours in the Tally-house, though the hours slip: he opens at the first bell of Aubade, which can be heard at the Ring on a still morning, and closes when the Arbiter's horn ends the day's last duel. He does not sleep. At night he reconciles. Every obol the Truce has ever struck passes through his cellar, and he knows the weight of each one to a grain; when a forged obol turns up anywhere on the Dial, it is Ledger who names the forger, usually within the week."], ["p", "His largest debtor is the Court of Dusk. In the hungry year after the Fall, when Gloaming had no harvest, the Countess [Vesper](heroes/vesper) bought grain on credit from the Tally to feed her people through the Long Dusk. The Court has paid the interest every year for a hundred years and has never touched the principal. This year, for the first time, Ledger has written to her about it. He says it is routine. Vesper does not believe him."], ["p", "He is not without friends, though he would not call them that. [Pip](heroes/pip) delivers every bill he writes, and has never once been tipped; Ledger considers this a compliment to the boy's honesty and Pip does not. [Shiori](heroes/shiori) of the Hourkeepers says his book is the worst-written history on the Dial, and he says hers has too many pictures and no totals; they meet every year to compare records and part in perfect mutual contempt. And there is [Grandma Ironknit](heroes/grandma-ironknit). Fifty years ago Ledger asked to write her into his book: the only time he has ever offered to record a person rather than a debt. She knitted him a scarf instead. He wears it under the collar, every day, and it is the only warm thing he owns."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "One of the Long Dead, the only one who works for the Truce. Tall and thin, in long black robes with an oxblood panel and gold coin chains, a tall oxblood collar, the great book at his side with its red wax seal glowing, a long quill. Under them he is what he is: skin like old vellum, grey-gold and drawn tight on the bone, the cheeks and temples sunken, bone pale through it at the brow, the cheekbones and the jaw; deep dark sockets with an ember in each, the cold fire of the Long Dead who keep debts; long thin hands with bone at every knuckle; thin grey hair slicked flat to the skull, and no brows left. Wick keeps a living old man's face; the Truce's two old officers read as the living and the dead."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Dry, soft and very precise, with the patience of someone who has never once been in a hurry. He speaks of people as accounts and of violence as settlement. He is unfailingly polite. He never raises his voice; he raises the interest."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"No, no. Don't pay now. I insist.\"", "**Picked:** \"Let us open an account.\"", "**Winning:** \"Settled. With interest.\"", "**Winning:** \"Paid in full. Sign here.\"", "**Losing:** \"Carried forward.\"", "**Losing:** \"A temporary imbalance.\"", "**Meeting Vesper:** \"Countess. About the principal.\"", "**Meeting Grandma Ironknit:** \"Still warm, madam. Thank you.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["He is still looking for the missing coin from the winter he died. He has started to suspect that it was one of the obols of the Truce, struck forty years after his death, which is impossible.", "The Tally's page for the duel of year 90 is blank. He remembers writing it. He has checked the ink: the page was never written on.", "If he ever balances the Tally to the last obol, his Reason is finished and he will lie down. He keeps one small debt open on purpose: his own, to Ironknit, for the scarf."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To audit it. If the Tower has taken realms, people, a sun and a market full of the Dial's dead, as half the Ring believes, none of it has been written down. And to know who won in year 90."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Vesper](heroes/vesper)**, owed by her: the Court's long nights were bought on credit from the Tally, and Ledger holds the note, with interest.", "**[Pip](heroes/pip)**, comrade: Pip delivers every bill Ledger writes, and has never once been tipped.", "**[Grandma Ironknit](heroes/grandma-ironknit)**, old flame: fifty years ago he asked to write her into his book; she knitted him a scarf instead, which he still wears.", "**[Shiori](heroes/shiori)**, rival: two keepers of the record; she says his book has no pictures, he says hers has no totals."]]]}, "heroes/mum": {"title": "Mum, the Silent One", "sub": "The Court of Dusk · [Aubade folk](peoples/aubade) · from [Gloaming](realms/gloaming)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Mum was born in the bell quarter of Aubade, twenty-six years before the Fall, into a family of ringers. Her name then was not Mum; it was a long Aubade bell-name that she has not used for a hundred years and which the codex will not print, because she would not want it. She sang before she spoke, the family says, and she sang so purely that by sixteen the choir had taken her, the youngest soprano in its history. The Sun Warden, the choir said, turned his head when she came in."], ["p", "She sang the Hymn of Noon at the Tower's top every noon for ten years, at the choir master's right hand, with a young baritone called [Cantor](heroes/cantor) at his left and a tenor called Ansel at her other side. The master liked her. In the last week before the Fall, the week before midsummer, he did something he had never done: he taught her the Hymn's last verse, which only the master knew and which was sung only at midsummer, in case, he said, anything happened to him. She learned it in three days. She was to sing it with him for the first time on midsummer day."], ["p", "On the day of the Fall the choir sang the Hymn at noon, and somewhere in the second line, Mum believes, she was a hair behind the beat. She had been thinking about the last verse. She felt the beat slip under her, and tried to catch it, and the sun stopped overhead and cracked and fell. When the light came back the Sun Court was empty except for the Warden, Cantor and herself, and she was on her knees, and she had nothing to say. She came down the Tower's stair beside Cantor in the dark, and she has not made a sound since. Not a word, not a note, not a laugh, not a cry."], ["p", "She came down from the Tower in a porcelain mask she made in the first month, to hide a face she could not control, and left Aubade within the year, because every bell in the city rang the hundredth short for the choir she believed she had failed. She walked the night roads west, and found a home at the Court of Dusk, which values people who say nothing. The Court made her a harlequin, a player of its revels, and in time the first of them. She plays the Court's revels in her coat of ivory diamonds; she reads its secrets from faces across a ballroom; she carries its messages without speaking them. Nobody at court has heard her laugh. The Countess trusts her absolutely, because she has never told anyone anything."], ["p", "In the Ring her walls and her ball cannot be seen, only the chalk lines she leaves on the floor, as if the world had to be drawn back in by hand. Her workings are silence: a wall that is not there until you walk into it, a ball you only see when it hits you. The Arbiter has had to rule, more than once, that a wall nobody can see is still a wall. The mirage archer, the archer of the mirage, builds walls nobody can trust, and each calls the other a cheat; they have fought in the Ring seven times and never once looked at each other. And the Hallbreaker, the sapper of the Underforge, has broken every wall she ever met except Mum's, which she cannot find, and takes it as a personal insult."], ["p", "Cantor still sings to her. Every midwinter he comes to Gloaming for the Masque of Houses and sings under her window, every verse of the Hymn but the last. She has never answered. She has never left the room."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Aubade folk of the bell quarter, though nobody can see it: Noon-touched, twenty-six at the Fall, she looks forty-six, narrow-shouldered in a dark violet coat with ivory diamonds and an ivory ruff, a porcelain mask in a dark hood with dark eyes, gold tear lines and a gold brow diamond, a long violet ribbon behind her shoulder, and big pale gloves with gold cuffs."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "None. She speaks with her hands, in the harlequins' sign, and with chalk. Her voice lines are gestures, written in brackets. When she is truly angry she becomes completely still."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"(Nothing. A pale glove rises, palm out. The wall is already there.)\"", "**Picked:** \"(She bows, very low, and does not come back up until you look away.)\"", "**Winning:** \"(She draws a chalk line under you, and dusts her gloves.)\"", "**Winning:** \"(A slow, silent round of applause, for you, from three feet away.)\"", "**Losing:** \"(She mimes a door closing. Gently.)\"", "**Losing:** \"(She lies down exactly where she fell, and folds her hands.)\"", "**Meeting Cantor:** \"(She raises one gloved finger to the lips of the mask, and turns her head away, and waits.)\"", "**Meeting the mirage archer:** \"(She knocks twice on the air between them. It answers.)\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["She believes she was the hair behind the beat, and that the sun fell because of her. Cantor believes it was Ansel. She has never told him.", "She knows the Hymn's last verse. She has never sung it, even in her head. She is afraid of what will happen if she does.", "The porcelain mask has a crack behind the left eye. She made it in the first month, and the crack was there from the start."]], ["h", 2, "What she wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To climb to the Sun Court and stand where she knelt, and decide there whether to sing. She has not decided."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Cantor](heroes/cantor)**, old flame: they sang the Hymn of Noon side by side on the day it failed. She has made no sound since; he has not stopped singing to her."]]]}, "heroes/nyx": {"title": "Nyx, the Umbral Stalker", "sub": "The Court of Dusk · [the Fallborn](peoples/fallborn), of the Cast · from [Gloaming](realms/gloaming)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "At noon on midsummer day a hundred years ago, as the sun stopped over the Tower, its last light came across the Dial low and hard, and threw every shadow long. In Gloaming, at the westernmost edge of the light, a woman was standing at the foot of the Night Temple's west wall, and her shadow fell across the wall, tall and thin, a hand raised to shade her eyes. The sun cracked. The light changed. The woman walked away. Her shadow did not."], ["p", "It stood on the wall for the rest of that first long dusk, while the Gloaming folk lit their lamps and the Court gathered to keep the Naming, and at midnight, during the Naming, it stepped off the wall. The priests who saw it say it stood in the forecourt for a long time, looking at its hands, as if it had never had hands before. Then it looked at the dark mirror in the temple, which reflects nothing, and nothing looked back, and it seemed pleased. That was Nyx, the first of the Cast."], ["p", "Nobody knew what she was. The Hourkeepers, when they heard, came to measure her, and she let them, and they wrote \"Fallborn: of the Cast\", and could say no more than that. She had no name, no voice for the first year, no memory of the woman whose shadow she had been, and no shadow of her own. She faded when she was left alone and came back sharper when someone looked for her. She did not eat or sleep. She watched."], ["p", "The person she watched most was the Countess. Vesper, in the hungry winter of the first year, was the only noble in Gloaming who walked the night roads alone to bring food to her tenants, and the only one who looked straight at the thing standing in the temple forecourt and said good evening to it. Nyx followed her home. She has stood in Vesper's shadow ever since: not the shadow Vesper casts, which is her own, but beside it, close enough that in some lights the Countess seems to have two. When the Court of Dusk was founded, Nyx became its knife: the Countess's guard, the Court's assassin, the one who hunts whoever the Court names. She chose her own name from the old Gloaming word for the hour after the last lamp."], ["p", "She wears matte black with a violet edge and nothing that could catch the light, a hood over a face that is not there, a slit of mint where eyes would be. In the Ring she fades when she is left alone, and her first strike out of the fade is the one that counts. She hunts mostly in the thickets of the Walking Wood, where the Court's enemies hide and the light is broken."], ["p", "She is not the only one of her kind, and that troubles her. The Hourkeepers count perhaps forty of the Cast across the Dial, most of them thin and confused, standing on the walls they peeled from; the Court has taken in the ones it could find, and Nyx trains them. A few have done things in the dark that the Court did not order. Nyx deals with those herself, quietly, and the Countess does not ask."], ["p", "Two people on the Dial can find her. [Lyra](heroes/lyra), the Hourkeepers' star archer, shoots arrows that draw constellations, and her stars are the one light Nyx cannot step out of: a shadow needs the light to come from somewhere, and starlight comes from everywhere. They have duelled in the dark more often than either has told anyone, and Lyra has won more than half. And the Huntmaster of the Moot, has tracked her through the thickets for ten years without finding a single footprint, which he takes personally. A shadow leaves no footprints. The Huntmaster says everything leaves a trail. Nyx has started leaving him small things to find, on purpose, to see what he does."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "The first of the Cast, the shadows thrown by the sun's last light that walked away ([the Fallborn](peoples/fallborn)). A slim figure of matte, light-drinking black, with a thin hard violet edge where the light catches it, a hood, and no face: a mint slit where eyes would be."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "A whisper, dry and very close, always seeming to come from just behind you. She learned to speak in the second year, from the Court, and speaks its courtly Ring-speech with no accent at all. She uses few words, and no names but the Countess's."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"You looked away. That's all it takes.\"", "**Picked:** \"I'm behind you. I'm always behind you.\"", "**Winning:** \"Lights out.\"", "**Winning:** \"You never saw me. Nobody does.\"", "**Losing:** \"Noon. I hate noon.\"", "**Losing:** \"I'll fade. I'll come back.\"", "**Meeting Lyra:** \"Put the stars away, archer. Please.\"", "**Meeting the Huntmaster:** \"Still looking at the ground, hunter? Look up.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["She knows who put the two Noonsworn bell-ringers to sleep on the night road in year 86. It was another of the Cast, a shadow that walked off a wall in Aubade, and that hates the dawn more than Vesper ever could. Nyx has been hunting it for fourteen years. She has not told the Countess. She calls it the Dawnless, and she knows where it takes its sleepers: to the Tower's roots.", "She has begun to remember the woman whose shadow she was. A Gloaming glazier, from the mirror quarter, the morning of the Fall. She does not know her name. She thinks Silvane does.", "She is not sure she can die. She is sure she can be forgotten, and that it would be the same thing."]], ["h", 2, "What she wants from the Tower"], ["p", "The Noon Seat kept empty, for the Countess's sake; and to stand where the sun stood and see what shadow it throws now."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Lyra](heroes/lyra)**, rival: the shadow and the star archer; Lyra's stars are the one light Nyx cannot step out of.", "**[Vesper](heroes/vesper)**, her ward: Nyx is the Countess's knife, and stands in her shadow wherever she goes."]]]}, "heroes/oblivion": {"title": "Oblivion, the Unmaker", "sub": "A loner · [the Fallborn](peoples/fallborn), the Unmade · from [the Tower](realms/tower)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "When the sun fell it tore the sky above the Tower, and something came through the Tear that the Dial has no word for. The Hourkeepers call it the Unmaker. The Ring calls it Oblivion. It does not call itself anything, and has never been heard to use a name."], ["p", "What is known is what was seen. At noon on midsummer day a hundred years ago, as the sun cracked and fell, a rent opened in the sky where it had hung, black, with a burning pink edge on one side and a fringe of cyan on the other, and a figure stepped out of the rent onto the Tower's top: a towering knight in black plate cracked with glowing pink rifts, a horned helm with one burning slit for an eye, a greatsword of void. The Sun Warden, at his post, turned to face it. What passed between them nobody saw. When the two singers of the choir came down the Tower's stair that night, they passed the knight on the stair, going down ahead of them, and it did not look at them."], ["p", "It walked out of the Tower's gate before the gate was shut, and across the crater of Noonfall, and into the War of the Banners. It fought for neither side. It walked across the Field, and where it walked, things stopped being able to end: a wound that would not close and would not kill, a fire that would not go out and would not spread, a battle that would not stop. The War of the Banners ended outside the Tower in year 2, on the Ninth Field. Inside the Tower, on the Climb's War chapter, it has never ended, and the Hourkeepers believe that is the Unmaker's doing: that endless war is the one thing on the Dial it has truly unmade. It holds the gate there, the War's gatekeeper, the only gatekeeper who is not of the realm it guards."], ["p", "It wants no throne. It does not want the Noon Seat, or the sun, or anything any faction can give. What it wants, as far as anyone can tell, is the Dial unmade, hour by hour, door by door: not destroyed, but undone, so that nothing can begin and nothing can end. While it stands still it pulls in whatever is near, and what it pulls in does not always come back. It has taken things from the Dial that nobody remembers having had. The Hourkeepers have a list. It grows."], ["p", "Between duels it is seldom seen. It stands, some nights, on the Ring's highest stand, under the Tear, quite still, with the shards of its cloak drifting round it, and the Ringside's people keep their doors shut. Sometimes it walks the Field of Banners, where the plough turns up bone, and the stones the people of Harrow have laid for the dead are found the next morning moved into a ring. Once, in year 77, it walked into the Walking Wood, and the Wood stopped walking for a month, everywhere, and the Moot does not like to speak of it."], ["p", "It comes to the Ring, which nobody understands. It fights under the Truce, keeps the rules, eats nothing at the Table, and leaves. The Arbiter has called its duels for a hundred years and says it has never once broken a rule, and that this frightens him more than anything else about it. Some say it fights because the Ring is the one place on the Dial where things end: every duel has a winner. Some say it is waiting for the gate."], ["p", "Three people have sworn to send it back. [Wick](heroes/wick), the Doorwarden, keeper of every door, against the thing that makes holes where no door should be. [Tempo](heroes/tempo), the unwound hour, who keeps the hours, against the thing that wants none left. And the Sky-Stitcher who sews the Tear shut every night and finds every stitch unpicked in the morning. It fights all three, in the Ring, whenever they ask. It has never once asked them."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "The only one of [the Unmade](peoples/fallborn) that has walked the Dial: a shape, not a body. A towering knight in black plate cracked with glowing pink rifts, spiked pauldrons, a ragged cloak whose hem breaks into floating shards, a horned helm with a single rift slit for an eye, and a greatsword of void."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Rarely heard: a low, flat voice with no breath in it, like a sentence read out with the punctuation removed. It speaks in questions. It never answers them."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"There was something here. Was there?\"", "**Picked:** \"Begin. If you can.\"", "**Winning:** \"Gone. Was it ever?\"", "**Winning:** \"Nothing. As before.\"", "**Losing:** \"Not yet. Not yet.\"", "**Losing:** \"An ending. How strange.\"", "**Meeting Wick:** \"Doorkeeper. Where does your door go now?\"", "**Meeting the Sky-Stitcher:** \"Sew. I will wait.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["It believes it did not come through the Tear by its own will: that something on this side pulled, at noon. It has been looking for what pulled for a hundred years, and has told only Wick, who did not believe it.", "It loses in the Ring sometimes on purpose. It wants to know what losing is.", "What it does not know: it is the evening of Senmere, the world the Tower kept before the Dial, the ending that world was never allowed to have, which the Tower drags behind it from sky to sky. What pulled it through the Tear was the Tower's taking of the sun.", "The Hourkeepers' list of things it has unmade includes, near the top, in Shiori's hand, a word: the old name of the Dial. Nobody remembers it. The word is blank."]], ["h", 2, "What it wants from the Tower"], ["p", "The Tower unmade, with everything else. Or, if the Arbiter is right that it fights in the Ring because the Ring is where things end: to reach the top, and end."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Wick](heroes/wick)**, sworn enemy: the keeper of every door, against the thing that makes holes where no door should be.", "**[Tempo](heroes/tempo)**, sworn enemy: one keeps the hours; the other wants none left."]]]}, "heroes/opaline": {"title": "Opaline, the Lightbreaker", "sub": "The Noonsworn · [Gloaming folk](peoples/gloaming) · from [the Deep](realms/deep)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Opaline was born in the mirror quarter of Gloaming, eight years before the Fall, the younger daughter of a glazier of the Mirror house. Her sister Silvane was four years older, and the two girls grew up in their father's workshop among sheets of silvered glass, learning to cut, grind and polish, to set a mirror so that it caught a lamp across a room, and to read faces in reflections. Silvane loved the mirrors because they kept things. Opaline loved the prisms that came in the workshop's crates from the Deep, because they let things go: white light went in, and colours came out."], ["p", "On the day of the Fall the light left Gloaming first. Opaline was eight; she remembers her sister at the workshop window with their father's best hand mirror, holding it up to the last of the sun as it went, and catching something in it, and covering it with her hand. Then the long dusk came, and the year without a day, and the change. Both sisters' eyes began to shine back the lamplight. Silvane said it was beautiful. Opaline said it was a cage."], ["p", "She left Gloaming in year 18, still a child to look at, with a bag of glazier's tools, and walked south along the night roads and the Brass Road to the Deep, where the light of the fallen sun had run down into the crystals. She wanted to see where it had gone. At the top of the Prism Stair, the stair of three thousand steps cut down through a vein of clear crystal, a Kilnborn glass-cutter took her in as an apprentice, and when he died she kept his house. She has lived there since, and gone down the Stair every day."], ["p", "What she does there is break light. The sun's light hid in the Deep's crystals when it fell, which is why the halls glow, and Opaline goes down and breaks it out again: she cuts a crystal open along its grain, and the white light that was trapped in it comes out as bands of red, gold, green and blue. She is looking for the one colour the sun has lost, the one that is missing from every band she has ever cut; she believes that when she finds it, she will know why the sun fell. The Noonsworn, who want every drop of the old light carried up for the sun, took her into the Friends of the Dawn in year 60, and she sends them what she frees. She is calm, pale and patient, with a ring of prisms circling her head and a voice that never rises."], ["p", "The miners do not like it. They hold that the light belongs to those who dug down to it, and [Mabel](heroes/mabel) of the mole-folk, who keeps the Lamp Road, has told her so, with her claws, more than once; they have settled it in the Ring three times and the Deep is still arguing."], ["p", "And her sister. [Silvane](heroes/silvane) stayed in Gloaming and became the Mirror Duchess of the Court of Dusk, keeper of its secrets in its mirrors, and still carries the hand mirror with the sun's last light inside it. Opaline went to the Noonsworn to set light free; Silvane stayed to keep it. They met for the last time in year 40, at the Masque of Houses, and quarrelled about the mirror: Opaline asked her to break it and let the light go, and Silvane said no. They have not spoken since. They have fought in the Ring twice, and both times walked away without a word."], ["p", "The Deep's light has changed her. She has broken more crystal than anyone alive, and the crystal has gone into her: there are facets now along her cheekbones and the backs of her hands, clear amethyst, which she cannot cut out, and which glow faintly in the dark. She is slowly becoming glass. She holds the gate of the Climb's Deep, where the halls are lightless, because she is the only champion who can make them bright."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Gloaming folk by birth, of the mirror quarter, and Noon-touched: eight at the Fall, she looks twenty-eight. She is slim and calm, pale with her people's cool cast, and her eyes shine back violet as every Gloaming child's have since the long dusk. Her hair is violet-black and cut at the jaw; she cut it on the Stair. The crystal has grown out of her in clear amethyst with a faint light inside it: facets along her cheekbones toward the temples, a ridge along each collarbone, clusters breaking through the tops of her shoulders (more on the left, her wand side), and small points on the backs of her hands. She wears a gown of deep amethyst open over cream, its neckline low enough to show where the crystal grows, a fine gold chain with a crystal pendant, the dark sash with its four bands of colour, and a glass-cutter's leather apron at the right hip with her father's tools in its pocket. The ring of prisms floats behind her head, and the wand with its bright crystal tip is in her left hand. Her sister keeps lavender and silver; the two read as the night and the Deep."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Quiet, even and precise, a cutter's voice: she says exactly what she means and then stops. She never raises it. People lean in to hear her, which she has learned to use."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"White is only every colour, waiting.\"", "**Picked:** \"Hold still. You're in the light.\"", "**Winning:** \"Broken clean.\"", "**Winning:** \"There. Every colour you had.\"", "**Losing:** \"A flaw in the cut. I'll find it.\"", "**Losing:** \"Light bends. It doesn't break.\"", "**Meeting Silvane:** \"Still keeping it, sister? Let it go.\"", "**Meeting Mabel:** \"It isn't yours either, Mabel.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["She has found the missing colour, once, in a crystal from the bottom of the Prism Stair: a colour with no name, for one heartbeat. She told no one. She thinks the same colour is inside Silvane's mirror.", "The crystal in her skin is spreading. She has calculated that she will be glass through in about thirty years.", "She still has the glazier's tools her father gave her, and polishes a small mirror with them every night, which she would never admit."]], ["h", 2, "What she wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To bring the light she frees to the Noon Seat, all of it, every colour; and to find out whether the sun lost its colour before it fell, or after."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Silvane](heroes/silvane)**, sister: two sisters of the glass: one breaks the sun's light free for the Noonsworn, one keeps it in a mirror for the Court.", "**[Mabel](heroes/mabel)**, rival: Opaline takes the Deep's light up to Aubade; Mabel wants it left for the miners who dug to it."]]]}, "heroes/pim-and-pom": {"title": "Pim and Pom, the Changeling Twins", "sub": "The Greenwood Moot · [Briarkin](peoples/briar), the Acorn court · from [the Walking Wood](realms/wood)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "In the first winter after the Fall, the winter of the Long Dusk and the Winter of Mud, the snow came early to the Frostfall road, and one night in the dark of the year someone left a woven satchel of birch bark on the doorstep of a stone cottage at the Walking Wood's north-eastern edge. In it, wrapped in moss and acorn-silk, were two children, a boy and a girl, perhaps a year old, with pointed ears and green eyes, asleep, holding hands. There was no note. There was an acorn cap on the boy's head and a blue leaf on the girl's."], ["p", "The cottage was [Grandma Ironknit](heroes/grandma-ironknit)'s. She took them in, and that night, while she was warming milk, she turned round to find the girl in the boy's place and the boy in the girl's, and the satchel empty, and both of them laughing. She turned back to the milk. When she turned round again they had swapped back. She understood then what they were, and why they had been left: Briarkin children of the Acorn court, born two for one, one life between them. Nothing like them had been born in the Underbough before the Fall. The courts were frightened, and left them on the doorstep of the one Briarkin they knew who had chosen to live among mortals, and hoped."], ["p", "Ironknit raised them anyway. She named them, since nobody else had: Pim for the boy and Pom for the girl, after the sound their feet made on her floor, which has not changed in ninety-nine years. They grew up in the cottage on the Frostfall road, among her spindles and her cat, with the Wood on one side and the road on the other, and every traveller on the road a new audience. They look ten and have for ninety-nine years: Briarkin children age as they please, and Pim and Pom have never pleased to age."], ["p", "They share one life between them. When one of them is hurt the other feels it; when one of them is out of breath the other breathes for both. They swap places whenever anybody looks away, and sometimes when they do not; they tag in and out of a fight as if it were a game, because for them it is. Pim is the one in the acorn-cap helm with the wooden sword, who runs in first and asks questions after; Pom is the one in the blue leaf hood with the slingshot, who stays back and laughs, and is the cleverer, and lets Pim think he is. Which one is which at any moment is a question they consider nobody else's business."], ["p", "The Moot keeps them because nobody else can. They have no court to go back to: the Acorn court has never asked for them. They sit in the Moot when they feel like it and speak for nobody in particular, and the Moot has learned to let them, because what they say, in between the jokes, is usually right."], ["p", "They came to the Ring at twenty, by count, which in their eyes was ten, and were given their first card by the Arbiter within the hour for swapping places mid-bout without leave. They hold the Ring's record now, thirty-one cards, every one for the same thing; [Referee Whistler](heroes/referee-whistler) has tried to explain the rule to them for ninety-eight years, and they have listened very politely, together and then one at a time. [The Great Zambini](heroes/the-great-zambini) adores them: they are his favourite audience and his worst assistants, and he has twice tried to saw one of them in half on stage only to find that he had sawn the other, and neither of them in half."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Briarkin of the Acorn court, born two for one. Small, quick and ten-looking, with long pointed ears, big green eyes of one colour, and nut-brown skin. Pim: an acorn-cap helm with an amber leaf crest, an amber leaf-collared tunic, a wooden sword with a glowing edge. Pom: a blue leaf hood with a glowing bead, a blue tunic, a slingshot with a glowing blue band and stone."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Two voices, high and quick, finishing each other's sentences, interrupting each other, and occasionally speaking in perfect unison, which is unsettling. Pim is louder. Pom is funnier. Both laugh at everything."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Pim: Tag! Pom: You're it.\"", "**Picked:** \"Pom: Which one's which? Pim: Guess!\"", "**Winning:** \"Pim: Got you! Pom: Got you twice.\"", "**Winning:** \"Both: Again! Again!\"", "**Losing:** \"Pom: Your turn. Pim: No, yours.\"", "**Losing:** \"Pim: That doesn't count. Pom: It counts a little.\"", "**Meeting Referee Whistler:** \"Pim: Card? Pom: Card! Both: Thirty-two!\"", "**Meeting The Great Zambini:** \"Pom: Do the hat one. Pim: Do the sawing one!\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["They remember their mother. She was of the Acorn court, and she did not want to leave them; the court made her. They know her use-name. They have never told Ironknit, so as not to hurt her.", "If one of them dies, they do not know whether the other will. They have decided never to find out, and have a rule that one of them always runs away.", "There are forty pairs like them in the Underbough now. Pim and Pom know every one, and visit them, secretly, on the Moot's nights."]], ["h", 2, "What they want from the Tower"], ["p", "To play in it. It is the largest house on the Dial, and nobody has ever played hide and seek in it properly. Pom also wants to know why children like them started being born, and thinks the answer is at the top. Pim wants to slide down the outside."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Referee Whistler](heroes/referee-whistler)**, rivals: the twins hold the Ring's record for cards, thirty-one, all for swapping without asking.", "**[Grandma Ironknit](heroes/grandma-ironknit)**, her wards: she found them on her doorstep the winter after the Fall and raised them as her own.", "**[The Great Zambini](heroes/the-great-zambini)**, friends: his favourite audience, and his worst assistants."]]]}, "heroes/pip": {"title": "Pip, the Wingfoot", "sub": "The Truce of the Ring · [Aubade folk](peoples/aubade) · from [the Ring](realms/tower)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Pip was born in the couriers' loft above the Ring's north stand in the eighty-first summer after the Fall, to an Aubade courier who ran the Dawn Road and a Coast boatwoman who ran the Truce's ferries. His given name is Pellam-of-the-Stair, after the Dawn Stair where his mother's family lived; nobody has called him that since he could walk, because he did not walk. He ran."], ["p", "He grew up in the Ringside the way the Ringside's children do: under the stands, in the kitchens of the Truce Table, between the legs of champions. He carried messages for coppers from the time he was six: a wager to the Tally-house, a love letter from a Coast oarsman to a Gloaming glazier, a challenge from a Dune house to an Aubade bell quarter. He was fast, honest and incurious, which is the whole of a courier's art: he never opened a letter, never lost one, and never asked what was in one, even when it was on fire."], ["p", "A challenge to the Ring is not real until it is delivered by hand, and the Truce's couriers carry them across the whole Dial: sealed scrolls from countess to king, from the Deep to the white north. At fourteen Pip was the youngest courier the Truce had ever sworn, in a courier's pack wider than he was. At sixteen he was the fastest. At seventeen he did the thing that made his name: a challenge had to reach the Hourkeepers' Council at Frostfall from the Dune Throne at Tamerit, corner to corner of the Dial, before the next midsummer's reading, which was two days away. Every courier said it could not be done. Pip crossed the whole Dial in a day and a night, through the Storm Canyon, round the Tower, up the Frostfall road and over the moors, and put the scroll in the Council's hands as the Nail rose."], ["p", "The Truce gave him gold wings for his circlet and his heels, and the title of First Courier, and the right to wear the Truce's colours in the Ring. He is the youngest of the Truce's officers and by far the fastest. He has never been paid on time, which he holds against [Mr. Ledger](heroes/mr-ledger), who writes the bills Pip delivers and has never once tipped him. They are comrades, in their way: Pip is the only person who goes into the Tally-house without knocking, and Ledger is the only person who knows Pip's real name."], ["p", "His rival is [Zephyr](heroes/zephyr) of the Spires, the fastest blade on the Dial against the fastest feet. They met when Zephyr, on the wind, overtook Pip on the Dawn Road and laughed; Pip caught him at the Waymeet and did not laugh. They have raced across the Dial every spring since, and the score is even, which both of them find intolerable."], ["p", "He sleeps in the couriers' loft where he was born, under a map of the Dial covered in pins, one for every delivery, so thick in places that the realms underneath have disappeared. He knows every road, every ferry, every short cut through the Wood that the Wood has not yet moved, and the hours each realm keeps this month by the Slip-sheet, which he learns the way other boys learn songs. The other couriers call him the Wingfoot, after the gold at his heels, and bet on him. The Truce has never asked him to slow down."], ["p", "He fights in the Ring to settle races, mostly, and once to deliver a challenge to a champion who would not take it any other way. He is not a great fighter and knows it. He wins by being somewhere else."], ["p", "Now he carries more letters than ever. Every faction is writing to every other about the Climb, and some of the letters he carries are addressed to people on the Climb's floors, people who have been in the Tower for a hundred years. He has delivered three. Nobody has told him how."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Aubade folk by his father, born at the Ring: young, wiry and freckled, with ginger hair and the eager face of someone who is always about to leave. A deep blue capelet, a leather courier's pack with sealed scrolls and a glowing seal, a gold winged circlet and gold wings at the heels, and a sealed scroll in a raised hand."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Quick, bright and out of breath, even when he is standing still. He talks in deliveries: \"for you\", \"sign here\", \"no reply needed\". He is very cheerful about bad news, because it is not his."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Sign here. And here. And there, where it's on fire.\"", "**Picked:** \"Special delivery! For you. It's a punch.\"", "**Winning:** \"Delivered. No reply needed.\"", "**Winning:** \"First again. Somebody write that down.\"", "**Losing:** \"Return to sender.\"", "**Losing:** \"Delayed. Not lost. Never lost.\"", "**Meeting Zephyr:** \"Spring's a long way off. Want to race now?\"", "**Meeting Mr. Ledger:** \"Your bills are all out, sir. Any chance of a tip?\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["One letter he carried was addressed in his own handwriting, from a floor of the Climb he has never been to. He has not opened it. He never opens letters.", "He is afraid of standing still. When he stops, he can feel the slip of the hours, and it makes him sick.", "He let Zephyr win one race, the year Zephyr's mother died. Zephyr does not know."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To run it, top to bottom, faster than anyone. And to deliver the letter in his pack to whoever it is for."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Mr. Ledger](heroes/mr-ledger)**, comrade: Pip delivers every bill Ledger writes, and has never once been tipped.", "**[Zephyr](heroes/zephyr)**, rival: the fastest feet against the fastest blade; they race across the Dial every spring and the score is even."]]]}, "heroes/professor-puff": {"title": "Professor Puff, the Aeronaut", "sub": "The Windward · [Coast folk](peoples/coast) · from [the Drowned Coast](realms/coast)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Professor Puff was born on the Kite Spire forty years before the Fall, under a name that is in the Spires' register and that he will not say. He was a small, round, serious child who did not like heights, which on the Spires is a kind of disability, and who solved it the way he has solved everything since: by studying it. At twelve he had read every book on the air in the Spires' library. At twenty he had read every book on the air in the frozen library at Frostfall, where the Hourkeepers made him a Correspondent. At twenty-five he built his first balloon, a bladder of oiled silk filled with a gas he had distilled from the rose-coloured salt of the Spires' lowest rooms, and went up in it, and was very sick, and came down having mapped the winds over the Drowned Coast to a height nobody had reached."], ["p", "He has been mapping the sky ever since. Before the Fall he mapped it from a basket under glass spheres of his glowing rose gas, and published the first true chart of the Dial's winds, the **Puff Atlas**, which every Windward captain carried. The Spires gave him the title of Professor, which he has used instead of his name since. He was forty and aloft over the Lantern Coast at noon on midsummer day, taking readings, when the sun stopped and cracked and fell, and he recorded the moment with an instrument for the first and only time in history: the wind changed direction nine times in nine heartbeats. The page is in the frozen library."], ["p", "Since the Fall the sky has changed, and he is mapping it again. The old atlas is useless; the winds keep no seasons. He goes up every week from whichever Spire has room for his mooring, and comes down with readings, and the readings never agree. He is completely serious about it, about everything; he has been called a comic figure, by people who have not read his work, and he finds this puzzling rather than hurtful. He is a small round scholar of the air in an aubergine coat with a high collar and two rows of brass buttons, goggles up on his brow, silver tufts of hair, big calm eyes, and a brass tank on his back. Above and beside his head float glowing rose spheres of gas in brass cradles."], ["p", "His gas makes whatever it touches swell, which makes enemies larger and easier to hit, and which, in year 55, floated a whole hive-tree out of the Walking Wood. He was mapping the sky over the Wood's south and let a bubble of gas drift down onto the great hive-tree called the Amber Mother; it swelled, lifted its roots and floated a mile out of the Wood before it came down upside down in a field of the Field of Banners, with the Hive court inside. [Sirix](heroes/sirix), the Swarm Warden, has not forgiven him, and the swarm rises to meet him whenever he flies over the Wood. He has written Sirix eleven letters of apology, with diagrams."], ["p", "He argues engines with [Ada](heroes/ada) of the Underforge at every Truce dinner: two inventors at one table; she wins every argument about engines, he wins every one about flying, and they have never agreed whether a balloon is an engine. And [Barry Cloudsley](heroes/barry-cloudsley) is his oldest flying companion: one reads the sky, the other maps it. They have gone up together hundreds of times, Barry calling the wind and the Professor steering into it."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Coast folk of the Spires, Noon-touched: forty at the Fall, he looks sixty, small and round with silver tufts of hair, brass goggles up on the brow and big calm eyes; the aubergine coat with two rows of brass buttons, a brass tank on his back, and glowing rose gas spheres in brass cradles above and beside his head."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Precise, polite and academic, with long words and footnotes spoken aloud. He explains things during fights. He is never sarcastic and does not understand when others are."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Hold your breath. No, really. It helps.\"", "**Picked:** \"A brief experiment. Do keep still.\"", "**Winning:** \"Hypothesis confirmed.\"", "**Winning:** \"Up, up, and down. As predicted.\"", "**Losing:** \"A setback. Noted.\"", "**Losing:** \"Deflated. Temporarily.\"", "**Meeting Sirix:** \"My dear warden, about the hive. I brought diagrams.\"", "**Meeting Ada:** \"A balloon is an engine, Ada. A very slow one.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["The wind that changed direction nine times in nine heartbeats at the Fall was blowing toward the Tower each time, from nine places. He believes the Tower breathed in. He has told only the Hourkeepers.", "His rose gas is distilled from salt found only in the Spires' lowest rooms, the ones nobody built. He does not know what it is, and has never said so.", "He is still afraid of heights."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To map it, inside and out, top to bottom, every floor's sky. Each floor has its own weather, and he has never been allowed to bring the basket in."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Sirix](heroes/sirix)**, rival: Puff's gas once floated a whole hive-tree out of the Wood, and the swarm remembers his face.", "**[Ada](heroes/ada)**, rival: two inventors at one table; she wins every argument about engines, he wins every one about flying.", "**[Barry Cloudsley](heroes/barry-cloudsley)**, friend: his oldest flying companion: one reads the sky, the other maps it."]]]}, "heroes/referee-whistler": {"title": "Referee Whistler, the Arbiter", "sub": "The Truce of the Ring · [Kilnborn](peoples/kiln) · from [the Ring](realms/tower)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Whistler was born in the masons' halls of the Deep, fourteen years before the Fall, the youngest son of a Kilnborn stonecutter who was not yet grey, because nobody was yet. He was small, quick and argumentative, and his father despaired of him: he could not keep a line straight, but he could tell you exactly where everyone else's line was crooked. When he was twelve his father took a contract to cut paving for the midsummer market at the Tower's foot, and brought the boy along to sweep."], ["p", "He was sweeping the straw-rope wrestling circle at the market's edge, the circle where a giant shrine boy called Yoko was warming up, when the sun stopped over the Tower, cracked, and fell. He remembers the sound, and then the silence, and then the dust. His father was in the market. He did not come back. Whistler stayed at the circle because Yoko stayed at it, and because there was nowhere else to go."], ["p", "Through the two years of the War of the Banners the circle was the one place near the Tower where nobody fought. Champions came to it instead: a knight of the crimson and a knight of the azure who agreed to settle a village's fate one against one, a pair of captains, a Ninefold who wanted the practice. Whistler swept the sand between bouts, and he watched. He watched every one of them cheat. The crimson knight stepped over the rope; the azure knight threw sand; the Ninefold spent a life and claimed he had not. Whistler wrote it all down on a slate, in the masons' marks his father had taught him, because somebody should."], ["p", "When the realms came to the circle to swear the Truce in the midsummer of year 2, the founders argued for nine days about who should judge the duels. Every realm wanted its own judge. On the ninth day a Coast captain asked the boy with the slate what he thought, mostly as a joke. Whistler read them his list. Every founder in the circle was on it. They laughed. Then they stopped laughing and made him Arbiter, on the grounds that he was the only person at the Tower's foot whom no realm owned, and because none of them wanted to be the one who said no. He was fourteen, and Noon-touched, and he has been fourteen-going-on-thirty-four for a hundred years."], ["p", "He has not laughed much since. He wears the black and ivory tabard so that no realm can claim his colour, and a black helm with a gold band and a red crest so that every champion in the Ring can find him. His silver horn, the Truce's gift, is the one sound both armies of the Banners still stop for: in the Climb's War chapter the dead soldiers lower their spears when he blows it. His sigil tablets carry the Truce's rules, each cut in stone in the masons' marks: when he holds one up, that rule binds whoever stands under it. He does not fight to win. He fights because a rule nobody enforces is only a rumour, and he has a long list of rumours to put down."], ["p", "The Ring knows two things about him that he would rather it did not. The first is [The Great Zambini](heroes/the-great-zambini), whose every duel Whistler has watched and whom he has never once caught cheating, which convinces him that Zambini always does. The second is [Pim and Pom](heroes/pim-and-pom), who hold the Ring's record for cards: thirty-one, all for swapping without asking, which they do not consider cheating and he does. He has tried to explain the rule to them for ninety-eight years. They look ten. He looks thirty-four. He is not sure who is winning."], ["p", "The grey came on him in his twenties, by count, long after he had left the Deep: Kilnborn skin turns in a generation, and his did, even at the Ring, as if the Deep would not let him go. He keeps the helm on in the Ring and does not discuss it."], ["p", "Now, in the hundredth summer, he has a new worry. The Tally's page for the duel that opened the Tower's gate in year 90 is blank. He called that duel. He remembers calling it. He cannot remember who won."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Kilnborn, born in the masons' halls of the Deep before the Fall and grey since his twenties: short, square and solid, ash-grey skin, pale amber eyes, a big calm square face. Dark armoured shoulders with gold-edged pauldrons, the black and ivory tabard, the silver horn on its gold chain, a red and gold sigil tablet with a glowing sigil, a black helm with a gold band and a red crest."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Flat, precise and loud enough to reach the back of the stands without shouting. He speaks in rulings. When he is tired he speaks in numbers. He never says \"I think\"; he says \"the rule is\"."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"That is a dash. I saw it. Everyone saw it.\"", "**Picked:** \"Rules are posted. Read them. I'll wait.\"", "**Winning:** \"Foul. Upheld. Next.\"", "**Winning:** \"Written down. Witnessed. Done.\"", "**Losing:** \"Noted. Under protest.\"", "**Losing:** \"The ruling stands. So will I, shortly.\"", "**Meeting The Great Zambini:** \"Sleeves up. Both of them. Higher.\"", "**Meeting Pim and Pom:** \"One at a time. I am counting.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["He remembers calling the duel of year 90 that opened the gate, and remembers raising the winner's arm. He cannot see the face. He has started to wonder if it was his own arm.", "His father's body was never found. He thinks the Tower took him with the market, and he checks every face on the Climb.", "He has, once, let a foul go: in year 3, in an exhibition bout, a Coast girl stepped outside the rope to get round Big Yoko and pushed him out. It was a foul, and no fall. He let it stand, because Yoko looked so happy. Nobody else knows."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "For whoever reaches the Noon Seat to have earned it under the rules, every step. And to find out who won in year 90."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[The Great Zambini](heroes/the-great-zambini)**, rival: the Arbiter has never caught Zambini cheating, and has never once believed he didn't.", "**[Pim and Pom](heroes/pim-and-pom)**, rivals: the twins hold the Ring's record for cards, thirty-one, all for swapping without asking."]]]}, "heroes/sefu": {"title": "Sefu, the Dune Sovereign", "sub": "The Dune Throne · [Dune folk](peoples/dune) · from [the Sands](realms/sands)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Sefu was born in the Throne Hall of Tamerit twenty-four years before the Fall, the second child of the old king of the Sands, and he was never meant to rule. The crook was for his sister the Uncrowned, three years older, clever, cold and much admired, who was crowned queen at twenty-five when their father died, two years before the Fall. Sefu was the king's brother: the commander of the Throne's horse archers, a handsome, proud, ambitious young man with a gift for speeches and none for patience, who loved his sister and resented her in equal measure, and who had been told every day of his life that the sun was his father."], ["p", "He believed it. The kings of the Sands always called the sun their father, and on the day of the Fall, when the shadow stopped and the sun cracked and the Glass Flats turned to glass under its heat, the Sands said it had fallen there, into the dunes, which are still warm. Sefu, twenty-four, rode out onto the Flats that afternoon at the head of his archers and stood on the smoking glass and felt the warmth come up through his boots, and knew, he says, that the sun had come home to its children. The Sands said something else as well: that the sun had fallen in the year of the Uncrowned's crowning, under her rule, and that a queen in whose reign the Father fell had failed him."], ["p", "The Uncrowned joined the crimson banner of King Orlan in the first months of the War of the Banners, for a share of the sun's pieces; the Sands did not like it. In the spring of year 1 the river towns rose, the court turned, and the Throne's guard came to Sefu. He took the crown from his sister in the Throne Hall at midnight, with her own guard holding the doors, and declared himself the sun's heir: if a sun was to hang over the Tower again, he said, it should be his. She was driven out that night. At the gate of Tamerit, in the dark, she laid a curse on the crown she no longer wore: it would never stay on one head. And the captain of the gate, the young lion the Lion of the Gate, opened the gate and let her go, and Sefu has never asked him why."], ["p", "He has worn the crown for ninety-nine years, Noon-touched, a king who looks forty-four. The curse has passed it from head to head every time he set it down: to a regent while he rode to the Truce, to his heir during a fever, to a cousin at a festival. Every one of them who wore it too long has fallen: the regent from a horse, the heir to the fever, the cousin from a balcony. Sefu has not fallen. The Sands say it is because he is the sun's true heir, and he believes it. He wears layered linen, a gold collar and a striped gold headdress, and carries a sandstone crook, and his crook and his sarcophagus Judge his enemies: a Judged enemy cannot run far."], ["p", "He holds the gate of the Climb's Sands chapter, the Storm Canyon in the great storm that blew on the night he took the crown, and in the Tower he sits in judgement at its end. He sends his champions to the Ring to win the climb, and wants the Noon Seat for himself. The Heartweigher, the Throne's judge of the dead, weighs the dead for his Throne, and once, at his order, weighed the living. [Auriel](heroes/auriel), the Noonsworn's Dawn Paladin, calls his claim heresy: who owns the sun, she says, is the sky. \"My father,\" Sefu answered, in the Ring, in front of forty thousand, and then, because he cannot help himself, \"you never met yours.\" She has not forgiven it. He regrets it, and has never said so. And his sister, the Uncrowned, loose again in the hundredth summer, calls him a thief. Each has sworn to see the other fall."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Dune folk of the royal house, and Noon-touched: twenty-four at the Fall, he looks forty-four. Dark bronze skin, a short beard, kohl to the temples and gold on the lids; layered white linen with a striped gold and turquoise headdress, a broad gold collar, gold armlets and greaves, and the sandstone crook in the left hand."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Rich, commanding and theatrical; he speaks in decrees and in the royal \"we\" when he remembers. He never asks. He pauses for applause, and is used to getting it."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Kneel, or be judged standing.\"", "**Picked:** \"The Father watches. Let us not disappoint him.\"", "**Winning:** \"Judged.\"", "**Winning:** \"The sand takes what the king gives it.\"", "**Losing:** \"Even the sun sets.\"", "**Losing:** \"We will remember your name. Briefly.\"", "**Meeting the Uncrowned:** \"Sister. The crown is heavy. Would you like to try it?\"", "**Meeting Auriel:** \"Paladin. Your sky is empty. My father is home.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["He has outlasted the curse because Vitriol brews him a draught every night that keeps the crown from taking him; it is shortening his life by a year for every month. He knows. He takes it anyway.", "He did not take the crown. He was offered it at midnight, by the guard, and he hesitated, and said yes because he was afraid of what they would do if he said no.", "He has stood on the Glass Flats every midsummer for ninety-nine years and felt the warmth. This year, for the first time, it was colder."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "The Noon Seat, for himself: to sit where his father sat, and become the new sun. He is not sure what that means. He is sure it means he will never have to set the crown down."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Auriel](heroes/auriel)**, rival: who owns the sun. The sky, says the Paladin; my father, says the King."]]]}, "heroes/shiori": {"title": "Shiori, the Last Brush", "sub": "The Hourkeepers · [Northerners](peoples/north) · from [Frostfall](realms/frostfall)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "The Hourkeepers write the Dial down before it changes. The frozen library under Frostfall Lake holds the record of every year since the Long Count began, in ink that the cold keeps from fading, and for six hundred years its finest writing has been done in the library's eastern script: an old brush-script from a far shore of the Outer Sea that a shipwrecked scribe brought to Frostfall in the library's first century, and which the Hourkeepers kept because nothing else could write so much so precisely. Its masters are called the great brushes. There were twelve before the Fall, and the work of a great brush, the Hourkeepers say, is not only precise but true: what a great brush writes comes a little true."], ["p", "Shiori was born in Hearthmere in the seventieth summer after the Fall, to a lens-grinder and a Reader of the observatory, and given a scribe-name at birth because her grandmother, who was the last great brush then living, held her and said so. Her grandmother taught her the brush at three. By seven she could write the library's script as well as any Reader. By twelve she could write a stroke that did not dry: her ink lay wet on the paper for a day, and the paper under it changed. By fifteen her grandmother was dead, and Shiori was the last of the great brushes, and the library's."], ["p", "She is the Hourkeepers' great calligrapher, the one whose writing comes true. She wears a rice-paper robe with its hem washed in ink and an indigo sash, long black hair held by a brush-shaped pin, and carries a brush as tall as she is, with paper talismans floating round her. She writes the year's book on the Longest Night. She writes the Slip-sheet from Tempo's clock. And in the Ring her strokes lie wet on the floor, and where two cross, the ink bursts: what she writes on the world, the world must read."], ["p", "She lives in the library itself, in a cell cut into the ice at its western end with a window onto the lake's underside, where the light comes through green and the fish pass. She writes there by lamplight eleven hours a day. The Readers bring her the year's records, and she copies them into the library's book in the eastern script, and once a month she walks up the stair to the observatory to watch Tempo's clock and write the Slip-sheet. She has left Frostfall four times in her life, every time to the Ring, every time for a duel, and every time she has come home and written the duel down before she slept."], ["p", "She has taught two people outside the library, both in a single year, and both have made something of it. The Kite Master of the Spires came north at fourteen to learn paper, and Shiori taught her to fold: a fold made once, cleanly, a figure from a single sheet, no cut, no glue. The Kite Master went home and folded a dragon. And the Golemwright of the Underforge came north at forty-five to learn a better script for his golems' brows, and Shiori, sixteen, wrote him one word; the golem it was put on stood up and bowed. The word on every golem's brow in the Underforge is in her hand now."], ["p", "She thinks [Mr. Ledger](heroes/mr-ledger)'s book is the worst-written history on the Dial: every debt and no story, every sum and no picture. He thinks hers has too many pictures and no totals. They meet every year at the Truce dinner to compare records, and part in perfect mutual contempt, and Shiori has copied every page of the Tally that he has let her see, in her own hand, with illustrations. And she works with [Tempo](heroes/tempo), the unwound hour: he keeps the hours and she writes them down, and both are always a little behind. She is the only person who can write down both of his sentences at once, and she writes them in two colours."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "A Northerner of Hearthmere, born after the Fall: thirty, with long black hair held by a brush-shaped pin, a rice-paper white robe with the hem washed in ink, an indigo sash, a huge brush in the left hand, and floating paper talismans."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Quiet, exact and a little severe, a teacher's voice; she speaks as she writes, one clean stroke at a time, and never corrects herself. She is funny in a dry way that only the library understands."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Hold still. You are being written.\"", "**Picked:** \"One stroke. No corrections.\"", "**Winning:** \"Written. And so it is.\"", "**Winning:** \"Your ending is legible.\"", "**Losing:** \"A blot. I'll start a fresh page.\"", "**Losing:** \"The ink is still wet. So am I.\"", "**Meeting Mr. Ledger:** \"Still no pictures, Ledger? How sad.\"", "**Meeting Tempo:** \"Slow down. I can only write so fast. Twice.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["What she writes comes true only a little, and only for a while. Except once: at fifteen, the night her grandmother died, she wrote her grandmother's name on the year's book, and her grandmother's ink has never dried. The page is still wet.", "She has seen the blank page in the Tally, from the duel of year 90. She has tried to write on it, with her brush. The ink would not take.", "The word she gave the Golemwright was the library's word for \"awake\". She did not tell him what it does to a thing that was never asleep."]], ["h", 2, "What she wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To write it down: every floor, every person, every realm the Tower holds, so that whatever happens at the top, the record is kept. And to read whatever is written at the top, because she believes something is."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Mr. Ledger](heroes/mr-ledger)**, rival: two keepers of the record; she says his book has no pictures, he says hers has no totals.", "**[Tempo](heroes/tempo)**, comrade: he keeps the hours and she writes them down, and both are always a little behind."]]]}, "heroes/silvane": {"title": "Silvane, the Mirror Duchess", "sub": "The Court of Dusk · [Gloaming folk](peoples/gloaming) · from [Gloaming](realms/gloaming)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Silvane was born in the mirror quarter of Gloaming twelve years before the Fall, the elder daughter of a glazier of the Mirror house, and grew up in her father's workshop among sheets of silvered glass with her younger sister Opaline. She was the serious one: tall, calm, watchful, with a gift for faces. She could read a customer's mood from his reflection before he had said what he wanted, and her father let her sell the mirrors because nobody ever left without buying one. She loved mirrors because they kept things: a face, a room, a moment, a light."], ["p", "On the day of the Fall she was twelve, at the workshop window, holding her father's best hand mirror, a small oval of Aubade silver in a frame of dark wood, while the light went. The sun was setting at noon over Gloaming as the shadow of the Tower lay across the heights, and she held the mirror up to the last of it, the way you would hold a cup under a dripping tap, and caught something. The glass went bright and stayed bright. She covered it with her hand. She has never let it out: its glass still glows pale blue, a hundred years later, the only piece of the old sun's light that anyone on the Dial keeps in Gloaming."], ["p", "The long dusk changed her as it changed every Gloaming child: her eyes began to shine back the lamplight, silver, and her skin cooled. She did not mind. She found that in the dusk she could see more in a mirror than anyone else could, and the Court of Dusk, when Vesper founded it, found a use for that. The Mirror house was her father's; when he died in year 30 she became its duchess, and the keeper of the Court's mirrors, and through them its secrets. The **Mirror Hall** of Gloaming is hers: walls of mirrors facing mirrors, in which, the Court says, the Duchess can see any room in the castle-city that has a mirror in it. She does not deny it."], ["p", "Her sister left in year 18, for the Deep and the Noonsworn, to set light free. Silvane stayed to keep it. They met for the last time at the Masque of Houses in year 40, and [Opaline](heroes/opaline) asked her to break the mirror and let the light go. Silvane said no, and has never told her why: that the light in the mirror is not just light, that it moves, and that some nights it looks back. They have fought in the Ring twice since, and both times walked away without a word."], ["p", "She fights with real mirrors, throwing them off walls and walking to catch them, and the one glass she will not throw is that first mirror, which is the only thing on the Dial that can turn the gaze of the veiled priestess of the Sands, whose look turns flesh to marble. The veiled priestess came to Gloaming as the Dune Throne's envoy once, and a guard of the Night Temple looked at her too long and became the stone-winged guard the gargoyle; Silvane held up the mirror in the Night Temple's forecourt, and the veiled priestess's gaze turned back on itself, and the envoy left Gloaming that night with her veil down. They have been sworn enemies since."], ["p", "She owes [Sir Whiskers](heroes/sir-whiskers) a life. In year 69, on the night roads, an assassin from a rival house put a crossbow bolt into the Duchess's carriage; the Ninefold knight riding beside it, hired for the evening because he was interesting, took it in the heart, died, and got up again three minutes later on his fourth life. He has never asked for anything in return. She has never paid it back. It is the only debt she has that is not in the Tally, and it troubles her more than any of them."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Gloaming folk of the mirror quarter, Noon-touched: twelve at the Fall, she looks thirty-two. Tall and calm, in a dark violet gown with a silver bodice and a gold edge, with a collar of looking-glass shards fanned behind her head, a calm pale face with blue eyes and sleek violet hair, and the hand mirror held out in the left hand with its glass aglow."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Low, cool and very exact, the voice of someone who has heard every lie in Gloaming and remembers which room it was told in. She asks questions she already knows the answers to. She rarely uses names; she uses reflections (\"you, the one who looked away\")."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Look at yourself. Now look again.\"", "**Picked:** \"Every room has a mirror. I've seen yours.\"", "**Winning:** \"That's how you looked. Remember it.\"", "**Winning:** \"Reflected, and returned.\"", "**Losing:** \"Cracked. Not broken.\"", "**Losing:** \"I saw that coming. I let it.\"", "**Meeting the veiled priestess:** \"Look at me, priestess. I'll hold the mirror.\"", "**Meeting Sir Whiskers:** \"I haven't forgotten, sir knight. I keep it.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["The light in her first mirror moves, and some nights it looks back. She believes it is not light at all but a piece of whatever looked out of the sun before it fell. She is the only person who has seen it, and it is the Court's third plan.", "She has watched her sister in the Mirror Hall every night for sixty years, through a small mirror Opaline polishes in the house at the top of the Prism Stair. Opaline does not know.", "The assassin's bolt that killed Sir Whiskers came from the Mirror house: from her own steward, who meant to make her duchess sooner. She found out, and dealt with it, and never told the knight."]], ["h", 2, "What she wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To see what is in the Noon Seat before anyone else, in her mirror, from a safe distance; and if what looks out of her mirror belongs up there, to decide whether to give it back."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Opaline](heroes/opaline)**, sister: two sisters of the glass: one breaks the sun's light free for the Noonsworn, one keeps it in a mirror for the Court.", "**[Sir Whiskers](heroes/sir-whiskers)**, owes him: he spent one of his lives for her, and she has not paid it back."]]]}, "heroes/sir-whiskers": {"title": "Sir Whiskers, the Lynx Duelist", "sub": "A loner · [the Ninefold](peoples/ninefold), a lynx · from [the Field of Banners](realms/banners)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "His name is Sir Wystan Aurelio of Nine, knight of the Keep of Nine, and he will recite it if asked and sometimes if not. The Ring calls him Sir Whiskers, which he pretends to dislike and secretly adores. He was born at the Keep of Nine on its ridge above the Field of Banners, twenty years before the Fall, a lynx of the Ninefold: tawny-furred, black ear tufts, amber eyes, and from the start extremely pleased with himself. He was the youngest knight of the keep to swear the oath, at sixteen, and he swore it, as Ninefold knights do, to a court: the crimson kings of Harrowmark, whose kingdom the keep stood in."], ["p", "He counts his life in nines, as the lynx knights of old did, and spends lives like coin: on a wager, a lady, a point of honour. He spent his first three before he was twenty: one on a wager that he could cross the Field's river on its winter ice (he could not), one on a lady of Harrow whose honour he had already compromised, one in a duel with a crimson knight over the correct way to hold a rapier. He was on his fourth life when the sun fell, and like every Ninefold knight alive that day, he felt his count go back to nine. The Noon Grace, the Ninefold call it. Sir Whiskers called it a second helping."], ["p", "He fought the War of the Banners for the crimson, as his oath required, and spent two lives on the Field, one in the first battle and one in the Winter of Mud, saving a Briarkin fox who had stolen a crimson captain's battle-cry and was about to be hanged for it. She stole the name of the life he had just spent, so that he could not remember what had killed him, which he considered the most charming thing anyone had ever done to him. That was the fox spirit, of the Fox court. He has been trying to catch her ever since, for longer than either will admit, and she has been letting him. When the crimson banner fell and King Orlan died in the mud, Sir Whiskers' oath fell with them, and he has not sworn to another court since. He has served many."], ["p", "He serves for the duration. A Ninefold who is between oaths takes service where it is interesting, and Sir Whiskers has been the champion of a Coast captain, the escort of a Dune caravan, the fencing master of a Gloaming house, the second of an Aubade paladin in a duel, and once, briefly, the Arbiter's deputy, which ended badly. He duels for whoever is most interesting, and lands on his feet. In the sixty-ninth summer he was hired for an evening to ride beside the carriage of the Mirror Duchess of Gloaming, [Silvane](heroes/silvane), on the night roads, because the Duchess had been told to expect an assassin and Sir Whiskers had said she would need someone interesting. A crossbow bolt came out of the dark at the carriage window. He took it in the heart, died, and got up again three minutes later on his fourth life of the new count, and finished the ride. He has never asked her for anything in return. She has never paid it back."], ["p", "He is an upright tawny lynx in gunmetal plate with gold filigree and a short wine-red cape, a gold gorget, a dark helm with his ear tufts through it, and a long rapier whose amber gem glows, and he has been thirty-one in this life for thirty-one years."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "A lynx of the [Ninefold](peoples/ninefold), of the Keep of Nine: a tawny lynx's head with black ear tufts and amber eyes, fur at the throat and hands, gunmetal plate with gold filigree, a gold gorget, a short wine-red cape, a cream halo of nine lives, and the long rapier with its amber gem in the right hand."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Rich, amused and theatrical, with rolled consonants and elaborate courtesy; he narrates his own fights. He never says a short sentence when a long one will do. He purrs, very slightly, when pleased, and denies it."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"En garde. And do try. It's so much nicer when they try.\"", "**Picked:** \"Sir Wystan Aurelio of Nine, at your service. Briefly.\"", "**Winning:** \"Touché. And again, touché.\"", "**Winning:** \"Landed on my feet. As ever.\"", "**Losing:** \"A life well spent. Was it? We'll see.\"", "**Losing:** \"One down. Plenty to go.\"", "**Meeting the fox spirit:** \"My dear. Shall we? I'll even let you run first.\"", "**Meeting Silvane:** \"Duchess. Still in your debt, am I? No, wait, it's the other way.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["He knows the Turncloak on the Climb's War floors. It is his brother, Sir Aldous of Nine, who swore to the azure, spent eight lives on the Field and went into the Tower on his ninth. Sir Whiskers has met him there. He has not told the keep.", "He has five lives left, not six as he tells everyone. He spent one in secret, in year 88, on someone he will not name.", "He has let the fox spirit steal his name once. For a night he did not know who he was, and was, he says, very happy."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To see what is at the top, because it is the most interesting thing on the Dial; and to bring his brother down before his ninth life runs out."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Silvane](heroes/silvane)**, owed by her: he spent one of his lives for her, and she has not paid it back."]]]}, "heroes/sirix": {"title": "Sirix, the Swarm Warden", "sub": "The Greenwood Moot · [Briarkin](peoples/briar), the Hive court · from [the Walking Wood](realms/wood)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "The hive-trees of the Walking Wood are hollow oaks the size of towers, full of amber wax and wasps, in the south of the forest where the sun used to fall longest; and inside them, in rooms of wax that the wasps build round them, lives the Hive court of the Briarkin. The Hive court is the least like people of all the courts. Its Briarkin wear chitin at the shoulders and the brow, see with faceted eyes, grow two pairs of thin wings, and think, the other courts say, a little too much like the hive: together, in order, without much argument."], ["p", "Sirix was born in the great hive-tree called the Amber Mother some time before the Fall; the Hive court does not count years, only summers of honey, and he says he was born in the summer the honey ran red, which the Moot's records put about twenty years before the Fall. He was a warden's child: in the Hive court, the wardens are the ones who leave the tree, who speak with the folk and the other courts, and who guard the flowers the hives need. He grew up flying (in short hops; the court's wings are better for steadying than flying) between the hive-trees and the meadows, learning which flowers would bloom when, and which folk could be trusted not to rob the trees in the first frost."], ["p", "He was a young warden when the Wood began to walk, and the hives woke. The wasps of every hive-tree came out at once, in the first hour after the Fall, and swarmed over the forest in a cloud that darkened the Wood's sky for a day, as if looking for the sun. When they came back they would not go into the trees. They gathered round Sirix instead, a cloak of wasps, and would not leave him. The Hive court made him its Swarm Warden that week, because the swarm had chosen. He says the wasps are not his: he is theirs."], ["p", "The hives fly where he asks, because he keeps the Wood's flowers safe, and since the Wood began to walk the flowers need keeping: a meadow walked over by a grove is a meadow lost, and the hives go hungry. He guards [Fenna](heroes/fenna)'s gardens because the hives need them, and works the Wood's edges with her, replanting. He wears black chitin plate with hexagonal pauldrons and a crested insect helm, which is not a helm, and a swarm follows him like a cloak. In the Ring the swarm fights with him: a barb, a sting, a cloud, a column of wasps."], ["p", "His friend is the moth priestess, the luna-moth priestess of the Moth court, who lays the Wood to sleep at night. The hive keeps the day and the moth keeps the night, and between them, the Moot says, the Wood's flowers are never alone. They meet at dusk at the edge of the night meadows, when his wasps go in and her moths come out, and talk for exactly as long as the light lasts."], ["p", "His enemy, though Sirix would not use the word, is [Professor Puff](heroes/professor-puff), the Windward's aeronaut. In year 55 the Professor, mapping the sky over the Wood from his basket, let a bubble of his rose gas drift down onto the Amber Mother, which swelled, lifted its roots out of the ground, and floated a mile out of the Wood before it came down in a field of the Field of Banners, upside down, with the whole court inside. Nobody died. The swarm has not forgotten his face. When the Professor flies over the Wood now, it rises to meet him."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "A Briarkin of the Hive court: black chitin plates at the shoulders and brow with an amber rim, hexagonal pauldrons, a crested insect head with faceted green eyes, two pairs of pale wings carried folded like a cloak, and a cloud of small striped wasps."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "A dry, humming voice with a buzz under the consonants, calm and literal. He says \"we\" for himself and the swarm. He is courteous to the folk and very direct, and finds jokes difficult."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Hold still. They only sting what moves.\"", "**Picked:** \"We are ready. All of us.\"", "**Winning:** \"The hive is satisfied.\"", "**Winning:** \"Stung. Swarmed. Done.\"", "**Losing:** \"The swarm retreats. The swarm returns.\"", "**Losing:** \"We are many. You are one. We remember.\"", "**Meeting Professor Puff:** \"The swarm remembers your face, aeronaut.\"", "**Meeting the moth priestess:** \"Dusk already. Good night, moth.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["The swarm that woke at the Fall did not come from the hives. It came out of the ground under the hive-trees, from somewhere below the Wood, and moved into the hives later. Sirix does not know what it was before.", "He can hear, through the swarm, every flower in the Walking Wood at once. Since the Climb opened, he can hear flowers that are not in the Wood. They are in the Tower.", "He is very fond of the Professor, and has never managed to tell the swarm."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To find the flowers he can hear inside it, and bring the hive to them. The swarm wants to go up. He does not know why."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Professor Puff](heroes/professor-puff)**, rival: Puff's gas once floated a whole hive-tree out of the Wood, and the swarm remembers his face."]]]}, "heroes/tempo": {"title": "Tempo, the Unwound Hour", "sub": "The Hourkeepers · [Northerners](peoples/north) · from [Frostfall](realms/frostfall)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Tempo was born in Hearthmere at the south end of Frostfall Lake, forty years before the Fall, the son of a clockmaker, and he was a clockmaker himself by twenty: a careful, quiet, white-haired young Northerner (the Northerners go white early) who could take apart any mechanism on the Dial and put it back together a little better. At thirty-five the Hourkeepers made him keeper of the great clock in the observatory's central hall, the clock that told the Dial's true time by the Tower's shadow on a brass dial fed by a mirror from the dome. It was the finest clock on the Dial. It had not lost a second in four hundred years."], ["p", "He was forty and standing at the great clock at noon on midsummer day, watching the shadow cross the brass, when the shadow stopped. He remembers reaching out to touch the dial, as if the clock had stuck, and then he remembers nothing, and then he remembers reaching out to touch the dial. He did it twice. Everyone in the hall saw him do it twice. He has done everything twice since."], ["p", "The Hourkeepers call him an **unwound hour**: a man who fell out of time at the moment of the Fall, and has lived every moment twice since. The truth is plainer: when the Tower closed its hand on the hour of noon he was, for once, a heartbeat behind his own clock, and it took half of him. He is not a ghost or an echo; he is one man, living twice. Every step he takes, he takes again a heartbeat later, and the first step and the second are both real. Every word he says, he says again, and both are heard, though most people learn not to notice. Every working he casts plays again. He has lived a hundred years since the Fall, every moment twice, which makes two hundred, and he looks forty, because the Noon-touched age slowly and he is not sure which of his two lives to count."], ["p", "He keeps the great clock still. Since the Fall it has had no shadow to read, and he has rebuilt it to keep the slip instead: it measures how far the Dial's hours have slipped from where they should be, and the Hourkeepers' monthly Slip-sheet is read from its face. He wears a long dark coat with an ivory dial plate on the shoulder, carries a staff shaped like a minute hand, and has a clock-dial halo, rings of radium numerals in chartreuse, that hangs behind his head and ticks. He races the dark through the Deep for practice, because the dark there has no hours at all, and he wants to know if he can outrun it twice."], ["p", "Living twice is lonely. His wife, a lens-grinder of Hearthmere, left him in the third year after the Fall, kindly, saying that she could bear being told everything twice but not being kissed twice and only feeling one. His friends grew old at the normal rate and died, and he attended every funeral twice. He lives now in a room off the great clock's hall, with two of everything (two cups, two chairs, two pens), and the Readers bring him tea, two cups, and drink the second one themselves so that he will not feel the lack."], ["p", "He has sworn to wind the Dial up again before [Oblivion](heroes/oblivion) unmakes the hours altogether. The thing that came through the Tear on the day of the Fall makes things unable to end; Tempo believes it is what stopped the shadow, and that it is slowly stopping time itself. They have fought in the Ring nine times. Tempo has won four, twice each. He works with [Shiori](heroes/shiori), the library's great calligrapher: he keeps the hours and she writes them down, and both are always a little behind. She is the only person on the Dial who can write down both of his sentences at once."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "A Northerner of Hearthmere, and Noon-touched, or something stranger: he looks forty, with white hair swept back, a monocle, a long dark coat with an ivory dial plate on the shoulder, the minute-hand staff in the left hand, and the glowing clock dial behind his head."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Calm, precise and doubled: every line is said twice, the second a heartbeat behind the first, which listeners learn to hear as one voice with an echo. He speaks in measurements. He is patient with everyone, because he has time."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Again. And again.\"", "**Picked:** \"Right on time. Right on time.\"", "**Winning:** \"Wound down.\"", "**Winning:** \"Twice as fast. Twice as fast.\"", "**Losing:** \"Out of time. For now.\"", "**Losing:** \"Again, then. Again, then.\"", "**Meeting Oblivion:** \"You stopped the hour. I'll start it again.\"", "**Meeting Shiori:** \"Behind again? So am I. So am I.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["The second Tempo, the one a heartbeat behind, is slowly falling further behind. A heartbeat in year 1; nearly two now. He thinks that when the gap is wide enough, there will be two of him, and he does not know which one will be himself.", "The great clock still has the shadow's last position on its dial, scratched into the brass at the moment it stopped. It does not point at noon. It points nine heartbeats past it.", "He has seen himself on the Climb's War floors, a heartbeat ahead, going up."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To wind it. If the Tower was the Dial's clock, it can be wound; and he means to find the key before Oblivion finds the last hour."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Oblivion](heroes/oblivion)**, sworn enemy: one keeps the hours; the other wants none left.", "**[Shiori](heroes/shiori)**, comrade: he keeps the hours and she writes them down, and both are always a little behind."]]]}, "heroes/the-great-zambini": {"title": "The Great Zambini, the Illusionist", "sub": "A loner · [Briarkin](peoples/briar), of no court · from everywhere", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "The Ringside has never known where The Great Zambini comes from, which is how he likes it. He says Aubade, Tamerit and Gloaming, on alternate days. He says thirty-nine, and has said thirty-nine for sixty years. He has played every court on the Dial under a violet spotlight, in a deep violet cape and a tall top hat with a gold star, and he has sworn loyalty to all of them, in writing, more than once, and he has kept every oath exactly: every one of them says \"for the length of the performance\"."], ["p", "What the codex can say is this. He is a Briarkin, and has never hidden it from anyone who looked closely: the ears under the hat are long and pointed, the eyes under the brim are violet from lid to lid, and he keeps the age that suits him, which is thirty-nine. He was born of the Fox court some four hundred years ago, and he did not leave it: it was taken from him. Three hundred years before the Fall the court held its midsummer revel at the Tower's foot, and the Tower took the revel's hour with him dancing in it. It was one night to him. In year 40 he walked out of one of the Hundred Doors under the Ring, laughing at something somebody had said behind him, into a Dial that had forgotten him; his sister stole his true name as he came out, so that the Tower could never call him back, and he has been a Briarkin of no court since. The courts are where the fae are remembered, and a fae who is not remembered fades. Zambini's answer, the only one of its kind, was to make sure he was remembered by everyone. He put on a hat, and became the most famous performer on the Dial."], ["p", "His art is glamour, the Briarkin's oldest craft: the art of seeming. He vanishes under hats, saws things in half and pulls the rabbit out last. None of it is a trick, and all of it is: the glamour makes the audience see what he wants, and what they see is true for exactly as long as they watch. The rabbit is real. Where it was before the hat, nobody has asked. In the Ring his workings are the same: hats he hides under, a box he saws, a spotlight that follows him, and an audience, which is what keeps him alive, though only the Briarkin know it."], ["p", "[Referee Whistler](heroes/referee-whistler), the Arbiter, has watched every one of his duels for sixty years and has never caught him cheating, which convinces Whistler that he always does. Zambini has never cheated in the Ring. He finds it very funny that he is the only person in the Ring whom the Arbiter is certain about. [Pim and Pom](heroes/pim-and-pom), the changeling twins of the Acorn court, are his favourite audience and his worst assistants: he has twice tried to saw one of them in half on stage and found that he had sawn the other, and neither of them in half, and the crowd thought it was the best trick he ever did. He thinks of them, privately, as the only family he has."], ["p", "He has tried, twice, to hire the mirage archer, the archer of the mirage, for the stage: a man who can stand a palm grove on the Glass Flats would be the greatest act on the Dial. The mirage archer says real magic does not take a bow. Zambini says real magic is the only kind that should. They have argued about it at every Truce dinner since, and Zambini has started to suspect that the mirage archer wants the job and is too proud to say so."], ["p", "He travels with a painted wagon drawn by two black horses that are never seen to eat, and plays wherever there is a crowd: the Ringside at midsummer, the Masque of Houses in Gloaming, the Homecoming fires on the dunes of the Sands, the bell festivals of Aubade, the Lake Opening at Frostfall. He plays for free to the villages at the Wood's edge, and charges the courts a fortune. He has never once played to an empty house, and on the one night, in year 64, when a storm on the Coast kept everyone indoors, he played to the Spires' gulls, and the Coast folk swear the gulls applauded."], ["p", "He fights for the applause. It is not vanity. It is survival."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "A Briarkin of no court, who has kept the age of thirty-nine for sixty years. A deep violet cape with a high collar, a tailcoat with an ivory shirt front, a plum waistcoat and a gold stud, a pale calm face with level brows and a small pointed goatee, a tipped top hat with a violet band and a gold star, and a wand in his raised plum-gloved hand with a glowing gold star."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Velvet, theatrical and delighted with itself: a showman's voice that turns every sentence into an announcement. He addresses everyone as an audience. He has never, in public, been heard to say anything plainly."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Pick a hat. Any hat. Wrong.\"", "**Picked:** \"Ladies, gentlemen, and whatever you are: the Great Zambini!\"", "**Winning:** \"And for my next trick: you, gone.\"", "**Winning:** \"Thank you, thank you. You've been a wonderful audience.\"", "**Losing:** \"Part of the act. Obviously.\"", "**Losing:** \"The curtain falls. Briefly.\"", "**Meeting Referee Whistler:** \"Check my sleeves, Arbiter. Go on. Both.\"", "**Meeting Pim and Pom:** \"My favourite assistants! Which half is which today?\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["He did not leave the Fox court; the Tower took him with its revel, and everyone he loved is still behind the thirty-first door, dancing. The fox spirit is his sister, and stole his true name as he came out, so that the Tower could never call him back. He has not forgiven her. She has never been sorry.", "He is fading. Slowly, in the empty hours between performances, his hands go thin, and he can see the stage through them. He performs more every year.", "His real age, by the Briarkin's count, is about four hundred."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "An audience at the Noon Seat: the whole Dial, watching, at once. If everyone on the Dial watched him, once, he would never fade again. He is planning the performance."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Referee Whistler](heroes/referee-whistler)**, rival: the Arbiter has never caught Zambini cheating, and has never once believed he didn't.", "**[Pim and Pom](heroes/pim-and-pom)**, friends: his favourite audience, and his worst assistants."]]]}, "heroes/vesper": {"title": "Vesper, the Crimson Countess", "sub": "The Court of Dusk · [Gloaming folk](peoples/gloaming) · from [Gloaming](realms/gloaming)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Vesper was Countess of the Gloaming heights before the Fall, the head of the Countess's house, the oldest of Gloaming's noble families and the one that held the night roads. She was thirty-five, widowed young, clever, and pale even for Gloaming; she liked the evening because her vineyards were most beautiful in it, and the morning because she could sleep through it. She was not a monster. Her tenants say she was a fair landlady and a terrible card player."], ["p", "On the day of the Fall the light left her lands first. Her castle stands on the westernmost height of Gloaming, the eleventh hour of the Dial, and when the sun stopped over the Tower and cracked, the shadow of the Tower lay across her vines and stayed there. For a year there was no full day in Gloaming, only a long blue dusk; crops failed, the old and the sick died, and the Gloaming folk changed, their eyes beginning to shine back the lamplight. Vesper changed more than anyone. She stopped needing the sun. Then she stopped needing bread. Then, in the hungry winter of the first year, she found what she did need, and it was blood."], ["p", "She has never told the whole story of that winter, and the Court does not ask. What is known is that the Countess's house came out of it with its tenants alive, its roads open and its lady not a day older than she had been at the Fall, and that she has not aged since. She is not Noon-touched; she simply stopped. The blood keeps her where she was, and she pays for every spell in her own and drinks it back from her enemies. She learned early to go low, close to empty, before she feeds. It is the only way, she says, to be sure you are still the one deciding."], ["p", "When the realms fought the War of the Banners she kept Gloaming out of it, and when the Truce was sworn she was the first of the northern nobles to sign, because she understood before anyone what the Truce was for: a way for a weak realm to survive strong ones. Out of the long dusk she built the **Court of Dusk**, the nobility of Gloaming bound together by one belief, that the dusk is the Dial's true age and the night has as good a claim as the day. She leads it, and she is its sharpest mind and its best fighter. Its long nights are bought on credit: the first harvests after the Fall were bought from the Truce's Tally-house, and [Mr. Ledger](heroes/mr-ledger) holds the note. The interest grows. The Court has never missed a payment, and has never once paid down the principal."], ["p", "The Noonsworn are her enemy because a new sun would burn her where she stands, and every one of her people with her. She does not want the Dial dark for its own sake; she wants it to stay as it is, with its long evenings and its quiet roads and its dead at rest under Gloaming, and she will do a great deal to keep the Noon Seat empty. She keeps the letter of the Truce and very little of its spirit, and the Arbiter watches her champions twice."], ["p", "Her hardest enemy is [Auriel](heroes/auriel), the paladin born on the first morning, who came to Gloaming in the eighty-sixth summer demanding a champion for two sleepers on the night road. Vesper came herself, because she thought she might lose and wanted to know what it felt like. She did lose. Eleven duels later they are even, and Vesper has said, privately, that she does not know what she will do with her evenings if one of them wins for good. The Sunbearer, who carries the Ember of Aubade on his back, is the other: the Ember's light burns her to come near, and she has sworn to put it out. And everywhere she goes, [Nyx](heroes/nyx) stands in her shadow, the Court's knife, who walked off the wall of the Night Temple on the day of the Fall and chose Vesper as the first person worth guarding."], ["p", "Now, in the hundredth summer, the Tower's gate is open, and the Court must climb too: an empty Noon Seat is not empty for long. Vesper has gone up as high as the Wood's crown, and come back with a look on her face that Silvane, who reads faces, did not like."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Gloaming folk, and the clearest case of what the long dusk can make of them. She looks thirty-five: tall and narrow, skin white with the blue-grey cast of her people, dark lips, black hair swept up under a small crown of red points. Her eyes shine crimson-violet in the dark. She wears a crimson gown over black with a high collar of blood-red blades, a gold edge, and a scepter with a blood drop in the left hand."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Low, warm and amused, with the manners of a hostess who has outlived all her guests. She never hurries a sentence and never wastes one. When she is angry her voice gets softer. Her laugh is real, and rare, and nobody in the Court likes to hear it in a duel."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Come closer. I'm already paying.\"", "**Picked:** \"The evening is young. So am I. Shall we?\"", "**Winning:** \"Thank you. That was very nourishing.\"", "**Winning:** \"Lie still. The night is long and you are not in a hurry.\"", "**Losing:** \"Ah. So this is what morning feels like.\"", "**Losing:** \"Put it on the Court's account.\"", "**Meeting Auriel:** \"Little dawn. You look rested. I could fix that.\"", "**Meeting Mr. Ledger:** \"Not tonight, Ledger. Tonight I'm a guest.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["The first person she fed on, in the hungry winter, was her own steward, who offered. He lived, and is still alive at Lantern-under-Hill, a very old man who will not hear a word against her.", "She has worked out that if the sun is hung again she will not burn at once, but age the hundred years she skipped in a single morning. She would rather burn.", "The night on the road with Auriel was her idea."]], ["h", 2, "What she wants from the Tower"], ["p", "The Noon Seat kept empty, and to see with her own eyes what is in it before anyone else does. If the Tower can be made to hold the hour at dusk, she wants that too, though she has not said so to the Hourkeepers."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Auriel](heroes/auriel)**, sworn enemy: the dawn, and the countess who would keep it from coming. Five wins each, and one night on a road.", "**[Mr. Ledger](heroes/mr-ledger)**, owes: the Court's long nights were bought on credit from the Tally, and Ledger holds the note, with interest.", "**[Nyx](heroes/nyx)**, her guardian: the Court's knife, who stands in her shadow wherever she goes."]]]}, "heroes/vitriol": {"title": "Vitriol, the Plague Alchemist", "sub": "The Dune Throne · [Dune folk](peoples/dune) · from [the Sands](realms/sands)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Nobody knows how old Vitriol is, because nobody has seen his face. The embalmers of the Sands take no name and show no face when they put on the mask: they are called by their trade, and Vitriol is the word for the oil the Throne's embalmers use to keep a king's heart from rotting before it is weighed. His hands are old: long, dry, stained to the wrist with the resins of the trade, steady as stone. His voice could be forty or a hundred. He has been the master of the embalmers' house in Tamerit since before the Fall, the court thinks, though the court's oldest members remember him only as he is: a dark hood, a pale glass beak mask with round green lenses, a long coat with a bandolier of vials."], ["p", "He is the alchemist who keeps the Throne's dead kings whole. The embalmers' house is a windowless house of white plaster in Tamerit's old quarter that smells of resin and myrrh, where the kings and great nobles of the Sands are prepared for the Valley of Lids: their hearts drawn and weighed against the Heartweigher's feather, their bodies dried in natron and wrapped in linen and sealed with oils that keep them for a thousand years. It is the oldest office in the Sands, older than the crook, and the embalmer has always been feared, because a man who knows every reagent that preserves also knows every one that does the opposite."], ["p", "Vitriol knows them all. In the Ring he lays one on you, then another, and lets them meet: a dart of toxin, a vial of fire, a splash of acid, a walking bomb of resin and salt that he calls a homunculus and the Arbiter calls illegal. He is fascinated by reactions: what two substances do when they touch, what a substance does to a body, what a body does to a substance. He is not cruel, he says. He is curious. He says it without any warmth at all."], ["p", "He served the old king, and then the old king's daughter, the Uncrowned, in the two years she ruled. In the last month of her reign, as the river towns rose and the court turned against her, the Uncrowned came to the embalmers' house at night and asked him for a curse: not a poison, a working, to lay on the crown so that it could never stay on the head of whoever took it from her. He brewed it. He has never told anyone what was in it. On the night she was driven out she laid it on the crown at the gate of Tamerit, and it has passed from head to head ever since, and she never paid him for it. He has not forgotten. The Uncrowned owes him, and the debt is not in the Tally."], ["p", "He serves her brother now, as he served her, because the embalmer serves the Throne and not the head under the crown. He brews Sefu a draught every night that keeps the curse from taking the king. It is shortening the king's life. Vitriol has told him so, once, and Sefu said thank you."], ["p", "In the seventy-first summer, on the Brass Road where it skirts the Walking Wood's south-eastern edge, he tested a new fume on a garden by the road: a mile of wildflowers planted, he found later, by [Fenna](heroes/fenna), the Moot's Grove Warden, to hold the soil where the Wood had walked. Every plant died in an hour. He was delighted, and wrote to the Moot to thank it for the use of the garden, calling it a successful test. Fenna has not forgotten. They have fought in the Ring six times, and she has planted a flower on every spot where he fell, which he finds a very interesting reaction."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Dune folk, by his hands, his trade and his tongue; nobody has seen more. A dark hood, a pale glass beak mask with round green lenses, a dark coat with a bandolier of vials of green and amber liquid, gloves stained to the wrist."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Soft, dry and clinical, slightly muffled by the mask; he describes what is happening to you as it happens, like a lecturer. He never raises his voice. He laughs, rarely, at results."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Breathe in. Now let's see what you're made of.\"", "**Picked:** \"A small experiment. Hold still.\"", "**Winning:** \"Fascinating.\"", "**Winning:** \"As I suspected. Mostly water.\"", "**Losing:** \"An unexpected result. Noted.\"", "**Losing:** \"The reaction is incomplete.\"", "**Meeting Fenna:** \"The garden thrives? How disappointing. For science.\"", "**Meeting the Uncrowned:** \"Majesty. About my fee.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["He is two hundred and ten years old. He has taken a drop of the embalmers' preserving oil every morning since he was forty, and it has kept him, as it keeps the kings; under the mask his face has not changed since the year he began. He does not know what will happen if he stops, and is curious.", "The curse he brewed for the Uncrowned cannot be broken by anyone but its maker. He could lift it tonight. He is waiting for her to pay.", "The fume that killed Fenna's garden was meant for the Tower. He wanted to see what it would do to a thing that drinks the Dial."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "Samples. Of the Heart of Noon, of Ash and Ember, of the Sun Warden, of whatever is in the Noon Seat. He wants to know what a sun is made of, and what it reacts with."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Fenna](heroes/fenna)**, sworn enemy: his fumes killed a whole garden of hers, and he called it a successful test."]]]}, "heroes/wick": {"title": "Wick, the Doorwarden", "sub": "The Truce of the Ring · [Northerners](peoples/north) · from [the Ring](realms/tower)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Wick was born in Hearthmere at the south end of Frostfall Lake, about fifty years before the Fall, to a family of lamp-keepers: the people who light the lamps of the observatory's long corridors and the library's caverns under the ice, and know every door in both. He grew up with a ring of keys at his belt and a taper in his hand. He was a quiet, thin, courteous boy who liked doors more than people, because a door, he said, is a promise that the other side is still there."], ["p", "He kept the observatory's doors for thirty years. He knew which ones stuck in the cold and which ones the Readers left open, which led to rooms the Hourkeepers had forgotten and which led to rooms they would rather nobody remembered. When the sun fell he was fifty and already grey-white, standing at the observatory's great door with a lamp in his hand while the Readers rushed past him to the Lens. He remembers that nobody shut the door. He shut it."], ["p", "In year 3, when the Kilnborn masons dug the foundations of the Ring's stands at the Tower's foot, they found beneath them a gallery of a hundred doors, already hung, already locked, opening onto places that were not under the Ring: the Sands, the Deep, the white north, the Wood's edge and further. Nobody had built them. The Truce sent to Frostfall for someone who understood doors, and the Hourkeepers sent Wick."], ["p", "He has kept the **Hundred Doors** for ninety-seven years. Champions come to the Ring from every realm of the Dial, and every one of them comes through a door that Wick keeps. He has learned what most of them do. Some open onto the realms; some onto places in the realms that are not on any map; a few onto rooms that are the same room on both sides; and one, the hundredth, he has never opened, and keeps a lamp lit in front of it day and night. He wears a long oxblood coat with gold braid down the front, the hood lying back on his shoulders, and carries a ring of keys with an amber gem among them that glows when a door nearby is open."], ["p", "He hates the Tear. A door has a frame, a hinge, a lock and another side; the Tear above the Tower has none of them. It is a hole in the world with no frame and no other side, and the thing that came through it, [Oblivion](heroes/oblivion), makes holes where no door should be. Wick has sworn to send it back through its own hole and close it after it, and he is the one person on the Dial who might know how to close a hole that is not a door. In the Ring he opens his own doors in pairs and shows his enemies out, politely, through the one they did not expect."], ["p", "Since the Tower's gate opened in year 90 he has been asked, many times, whether the gate is one of his. It is not. He has stood in front of it with every key on his ring and felt nothing answer. But he has noticed that on the nights after a champion goes up the Climb, one of the Hundred Doors is warm to the touch in the morning, never the same one twice, as if something on the other side had leaned against it in the night. He writes down which, and sends the list to Frostfall."], ["p", "His friend is the Sky-Stitcher, who sews places together with a golden thread: he keeps the doors and she keeps the seams, and they agree that the Tear is neither. They meet once a month at the Waymeet inn, where his family's old cousin keeps the fire, and compare notes on the Tear, which is getting wider. This year Wick has started locking doors that have never been locked."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "A Northerner of Hearthmere: tall, thin and old, with a long calm face, level brows and a thin smile; his white hair, which went white before he was thirty as Northern hair does, swept back. The oxblood coat, the gold braid, the ring of keys and the amber gem stay."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Soft, courteous and slightly old-fashioned, the voice of an usher at a very good house. He gives directions even when nobody asked. He apologises before he hits you."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"This way, please. No, the other this way.\"", "**Picked:** \"Mind the step. And the one after.\"", "**Winning:** \"You've been shown out.\"", "**Winning:** \"And the door closes. Thank you for visiting.\"", "**Losing:** \"Locked out. How very rude.\"", "**Losing:** \"I'll find another way in. I always do.\"", "**Meeting Oblivion:** \"You have no door. You have no business here.\"", "**Meeting the Sky-Stitcher:** \"Still sewing? It's wider today.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["He opened the hundredth door once, in year 4, for a moment. On the other side was the Ring, at night, empty, a hundred years from now. He has kept the lamp lit in front of it since.", "He believes the Hundred Doors and the Tower were made by the same hand, and that the Tower is, in the end, a door.", "He knows which door the Uncrowned used to leave the Gaol of Oaths, and that it is not one of his. It frightens him."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To find its other side. Every door has one, and the Tower is the largest door on the Dial. And to close the Tear behind Oblivion."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Oblivion](heroes/oblivion)**, sworn enemy: the keeper of every door, against the thing that makes holes where no door should be."]]]}, "heroes/zephyr": {"title": "Zephyr, the Wind Blade", "sub": "The Windward · [Coast folk](peoples/coast) · from [the Drowned Coast](realms/coast)", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Biography"], ["p", "Zephyr was born on the fourth of the Spires, in the seventy-sixth summer after the Fall, in a room so high that clouds came in at the window. The Spire-born are a lineage of the Coast folk who have lived in the broken sky towers for as long as anyone knows: lighter and longer-limbed than the harbour folk, with heads that do not spin at heights, and an old knack for the wind that the harbour folk call a working and the Spire-born call walking. A Spire-born child learns to stand on a ledge in a gale before it learns to walk on a floor. Zephyr learned to walk on the wind itself before he walked on stone: at three his mother found him standing on nothing, a yard outside the window, laughing."], ["p", "He grew up quick and bored. The Spires are small, and a boy who can run along the wind between them has run every path by ten. He raced the gulls, then the kites, then the storm fronts, and won. At fourteen the Windward's elders, who keep the old wind-forge at the top of the fourth Spire, forged him a blade from a gust: a curved sword of pale metal so light it seems to want to fly out of his hand, with a hot leading edge and a thin dark inner edge, that cuts the air in sickle crescents. Every dash he makes gives him another gust to spend. He calls it Gale. He named it at fourteen and is embarrassed about it now."], ["p", "He came down off the Spires at sixteen because there was nobody left on them to race. He found [Pip](heroes/pip), the Truce's courier, on the Dawn Road, and overtook him on the wind, and laughed; Pip caught him at the Waymeet, on foot, and did not laugh. They have raced across the whole Dial every spring since, the fastest feet against the fastest blade, Aubade to Frostfall by any road, and the score is exactly even, which both of them find intolerable. He races the Lion of the Gate of Tamerit, across the Glass Flats too, every year at the Homecoming, and loses less: the lion has never lost on sand, and Zephyr has come within a length of it twice. And he races climbers to the lighthouse on the Climb's Sea floors, for practice, and does not lose at all."], ["p", "He is lean and quick, in a short teal coat and a very long jade scarf, with swept white hair and a pink blossom tucked behind one ear that he never explains. He fights in the Ring because standing still bores him: in a duel he is never where the blow lands, and his crescents come from every side at once."], ["p", "He flies with the Kite Master who flies her paper dragon on his winds and whom he never lets forget it. They grew up on neighbouring Spires and sent each other messages by kite before either could write; she taught him to read the wind's shapes, and he taught her to ride it, and the two of them together carry the Windward's messages across the Spires faster than anything on the Dial except Pip."], ["p", "He has been up the Climb more often than any Windward champion, never to the top and never for long: he goes up a floor or two, looks round, races something, and comes down bored. He says the Tower's air is wrong, too still, and that its winds blow in circles; Professor Puff, who has asked him for readings, agrees, and has given him a small brass instrument to carry, which Zephyr has lost twice."], ["p", "His mother died in the ninety-fourth summer, in a storm at sea. That spring he raced Pip and won, and has never been sure he deserved to."], ["h", 2, "People and appearance"], ["p", "Coast folk, Spire-born, born after the Fall: twenty-four, lean and long-limbed, with swept white hair (the Spire-born go white young, from the wind, they say), a mask of jade cloth across the lower face, a short teal coat, a very long jade scarf streaming behind him, bandaged forearms, and the curved wind-blade in the right hand."], ["h", 2, "Voice"], ["p", "Fast, light and teasing, always slightly out of breath from laughing. He finishes other people's sentences because they are too slow. He never says goodbye; he is just gone."], ["h", 2, "Voice lines"], ["ul", ["**Picked:** \"Catch up. I'll wait. No, I won't.\"", "**Picked:** \"Wind's up. Let's go.\"", "**Winning:** \"Too slow.\"", "**Winning:** \"Did you blink? You blinked.\"", "**Losing:** \"Headwind. Happens.\"", "**Losing:** \"Next time I won't wait for you.\"", "**Meeting Pip:** \"Race you to the Tower and back. Loser buys.\"", "**Meeting the Lion of the Gate:** \"Same lion? Same loss. For you this time.\""]], ["h", 2, "Secrets"], ["ul", ["He knows Pip let him win the spring his mother died. He has let Pip win once since, to make it even, and Pip does not know either.", "The blossom behind his ear is from a tree on the Climb's Wood floors, which flowers out of season. He picked it on his first climb and it has not wilted.", "He is afraid of heights. Not of falling: of standing still at the top."]], ["h", 2, "What he wants from the Tower"], ["p", "To run it, top to bottom, faster than Pip; and to stand on the Noon Seat for one heartbeat, and then jump."], ["h", 2, "Ties"], ["ul", ["**[Pip](heroes/pip)**, rival: the fastest feet against the fastest blade; they race across the Dial every spring and the score is even."]]]}};
